var e=[{id:`head-face`,label:`Head and face`,hint:`Forehead, eyes, jaw, cheeks`},{id:`neck`,label:`Neck`,hint:`Base of the skull, throat, nape`},{id:`chest`,label:`Chest`,hint:`Sternum, ribs, side of the chest`},{id:`abdomen`,label:`Abdomen`,hint:`Above and below the navel`},{id:`back`,label:`Back`,hint:`Along the spine and shoulder blade`},{id:`shoulder-arm`,label:`Shoulder and arm`,hint:`Shoulder, upper arm, elbow, forearm`},{id:`hand`,label:`Hand`,hint:`Wrist, palm, fingers`},{id:`hip-leg`,label:`Hip and leg`,hint:`Hip, thigh, knee, calf`},{id:`foot`,label:`Foot`,hint:`Ankle, top of the foot, sole`},{id:`ear`,label:`Ear`,hint:`In front of the ear, not the ear canal`}],t=[{id:`pain`,label:`Pain relief`,hint:`Aches people often look up`},{id:`headache`,label:`Headache`,hint:`Temple, nape, forehead`},{id:`stress`,label:`Stress and shen`,hint:`Restlessness, worry, spirit`},{id:`sleep`,label:`Sleep`,hint:`Winding down at night`},{id:`digestion`,label:`Digestion`,hint:`Fullness, appetite, abdomen`},{id:`nausea`,label:`Nausea`,hint:`Queasiness, travel, fullness`},{id:`immunity`,label:`Immunity and colds`,hint:`First signs of a cold, surface`},{id:`womens`,label:`Women’s health`,hint:`Cycle discomfort in traditional terms`},{id:`energy`,label:`Energy and fatigue`,hint:`Tiredness, low vitality`},{id:`breathing`,label:`Breathing`,hint:`Chest, cough teaching patterns`},{id:`tension`,label:`Local muscle tension`,hint:`Neck, shoulder, jaw, calf`},{id:`ed`,label:`Erectile difficulty`,hint:`What people call ED. Traditional patterns, not a treatment.`},{id:`memory`,label:`Memory and focus`,hint:`Brain fog and forgetfulness, in traditional language. Not dementia care.`},{id:`worry`,label:`Worry`,hint:`A busy, uneasy mind. Not a treatment for anxiety.`},{id:`dizziness`,label:`Dizziness`,hint:`A light or spinning head in textbook language. Not a stroke check.`},{id:`eyes`,label:`Eyes`,hint:`Tired eyes and frontal strain.`},{id:`ears`,label:`Ears`,hint:`Traditional notes on ringing and the ear. Not hearing care.`},{id:`sinus`,label:`Sinus and nose`,hint:`Stuffy face and the start of a cold.`},{id:`low-back`,label:`Low back`,hint:`A tired or achy waist. Not an injury plan.`},{id:`constipation`,label:`Constipation`,hint:`Ordinary sluggish bowels.`},{id:`urinary`,label:`Urinary`,hint:`Frequency and damp, in old language. Not an infection test.`},{id:`cold`,label:`Cold limbs`,hint:`Hands and feet that stay cold.`},{id:`palpitations`,label:`Palpitations`,hint:`A fluttering chest in spirit language. Not a heart exam.`},{id:`joints`,label:`Joints`,hint:`Stiff or achy joints people look up.`}],n=[{id:`source`,label:`Source`,hint:`Yuan-source points`},{id:`luo`,label:`Luo`,hint:`Connecting points`},{id:`xi-cleft`,label:`Xi-cleft`,hint:`Cleft points, often for acute patterns`},{id:`front-mu`,label:`Front-mu`,hint:`Alarm points on the torso`},{id:`back-shu`,label:`Back-shu`,hint:`Transport points on the back`},{id:`five-shu`,label:`Five-shu`,hint:`Well, spring, stream, river, sea`},{id:`command`,label:`Command`,hint:`Points taught for a whole region`},{id:`extra`,label:`Extra point`,hint:`Outside the 14 channels`},{id:`opening`,label:`Opening`,hint:`Opens an extraordinary vessel`},{id:`influential`,label:`Influential`,hint:`Hui-meeting points`},{id:`window`,label:`Heavenly Gates`,hint:`Window of Heaven points`}],r=`Traditionally taught as a Window of Heaven point, one of the Heavenly Gates, where qi and blood are said to pass between the head and the body. Study language only, not a treatment.`,i=[{id:`release-exterior`,label:`Release exterior`,hint:`First-day colds and chills`},{id:`clear-heat`,label:`Clear heat`,hint:`Patterns that feel hot or irritable`},{id:`drain-damp`,label:`Drain damp`,hint:`Heaviness, puffiness, waterlogged digestion`},{id:`transform-phlegm`,label:`Transform phlegm`,hint:`Nausea, mucus, a thick stuck feeling`},{id:`move-qi`,label:`Move qi`,hint:`Bloating and a stuck feeling in the ribs`},{id:`move-blood`,label:`Move blood`,hint:`Fixed aches and stuck-cycle language`},{id:`tonify-qi`,label:`Tonify qi`,hint:`Tiredness and a weak appetite`},{id:`tonify-blood`,label:`Tonify blood`,hint:`Dryness and cycle depletion`},{id:`tonify-yang`,label:`Tonify yang`,hint:`Cold limbs and low vitality`},{id:`tonify-yin`,label:`Tonify yin`,hint:`Dryness, thirst, and night heat`},{id:`calm-shen`,label:`Calm shen`,hint:`Sleep and a restless mind`},{id:`extinguish-wind`,label:`Extinguish wind`,hint:`Dizziness and head tension, in textbook language`},{id:`stabilize-bind`,label:`Stabilize and bind`,hint:`Weakness that seems not to hold`}],a=[{id:`general`,label:`General acupressure`,hint:`Ordinary gentle pressure`},{id:`caution`,label:`Use caution`,hint:`Eyes, throat, arteries, lungs, or strong stimulation`},{id:`pregnancy`,label:`Traditionally avoided in pregnancy`,hint:`Not for self-care routines while pregnant`}],o=[{id:`wood`,label:`Wood`,season:`Spring`,climate:`Wind`,sense:`Eyes`,tissue:`Sinews`,emotion:`Anger, in the classical set`,flavor:`Sour`},{id:`fire`,label:`Fire`,season:`Summer`,climate:`Heat`,sense:`Tongue`,tissue:`Vessels`,emotion:`Joy, in the classical set`,flavor:`Bitter`},{id:`earth`,label:`Earth`,season:`Late summer`,climate:`Damp`,sense:`Mouth`,tissue:`Flesh`,emotion:`Pensiveness`,flavor:`Sweet`},{id:`metal`,label:`Metal`,season:`Autumn`,climate:`Dryness`,sense:`Nose`,tissue:`Skin`,emotion:`Grief`,flavor:`Acrid`},{id:`water`,label:`Water`,season:`Winter`,climate:`Cold`,sense:`Ears`,tissue:`Bones`,emotion:`Fear`,flavor:`Salty`}],s=[{id:`hot`,label:`Hot`},{id:`warm`,label:`Warm`},{id:`neutral`,label:`Neutral`},{id:`cool`,label:`Cool`},{id:`cold`,label:`Cold`}],c={acrid:`Acrid`,bitter:`Bitter`,sweet:`Sweet`,sour:`Sour`,salty:`Salty`,bland:`Bland`,astringent:`Astringent`},l=t=>e.find(e=>e.id===t)?.label??t,u=e=>t.find(t=>t.id===e)?.label??e,d=e=>n.find(t=>t.id===e)?.label??e,f=e=>i.find(t=>t.id===e)?.label??e,p=e=>o.find(t=>t.id===e)?.label??e,m=e=>a.find(t=>t.id===e)?.label??e,h=e=>s.find(t=>t.id===e)?.label??e,g={wood:`text-wood`,fire:`text-fire`,earth:`text-earth`,metal:`text-metal`,water:`text-water`},_={wood:`bg-wood`,fire:`bg-fire`,earth:`bg-earth`,metal:`bg-metal`,water:`bg-water`},v={wood:`border-wood`,fire:`border-fire`,earth:`border-earth`,metal:`border-metal`,water:`border-water`},y=`Educational reference only. Acupressure descriptions are for learning and gentle self-care. Do not needle yourself. Stop if pain, dizziness, or unusual symptoms occur. Seek licensed care for illness, pregnancy, or injury.`,b=[`wood`,`fire`,`earth`,`metal`,`water`],x=[`wood`,`earth`,`water`,`fire`,`metal`],S=[{id:`lu`,code:`LU`,name:`Lung`,hanzi:`手太阴肺经`,pinyin:`Shou Taiyin Feijing`,element:`metal`,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`li`,limb:`Hand Taiyin`,clock:`3–5 a.m.`,organ:`Lung`,classicalCount:11,themes:[`Breathing and the chest`,`Protective qi at the surface`,`Skin and the first sign of a cold`,`Grief, in the five-element set`],pathway:`Teaching charts start this channel in the middle burner, bring it to the chest at LU1, then down the inner arm across the elbow crease, over the pulse at the wrist, and out to the thumb.`,note:`Yin metal channel, paired with Large Intestine. The clock time is a memory aid, not a diagnosis.`},{id:`li`,code:`LI`,name:`Large Intestine`,hanzi:`手阳明大肠经`,pinyin:`Shou Yangming Dachang Jing`,element:`metal`,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`lu`,limb:`Hand Yangming`,clock:`5–7 a.m.`,organ:`Large Intestine`,classicalCount:20,themes:[`Face, nose, and teeth in channel teaching`,`Letting go, paired with Lung`,`Surface heat and pain along the arm`,`The bowel as a yang partner, not a lab test`],pathway:`From the index finger it climbs the arm to the shoulder, crosses the neck, and ends beside the nose. A branch is drawn linking it with the Lung channel.`,note:`Yang metal channel. Some schools write the same points without changing the numbers.`},{id:`st`,code:`ST`,name:`Stomach`,hanzi:`足阳明胃经`,pinyin:`Zu Yangming Wei Jing`,element:`earth`,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`sp`,limb:`Foot Yangming`,clock:`7–9 a.m.`,organ:`Stomach`,classicalCount:45,themes:[`Appetite and the middle`,`Face and jaw`,`Abundant qi and blood in classical language`,`The front of the leg`],pathway:`Begins under the eye, travels the face and jaw, down the throat and chest, through the abdomen, and along the front of the leg to the second toe.`,note:`Yang earth channel, paired with Spleen. The longest day-side channel in the usual count.`},{id:`sp`,code:`SP`,name:`Spleen`,hanzi:`足太阴脾经`,pinyin:`Zu Taiyin Pi Jing`,element:`earth`,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`st`,limb:`Foot Taiyin`,clock:`9–11 a.m.`,organ:`Spleen`,classicalCount:21,themes:[`Transforming food into qi, as a teaching idea`,`Holding blood`,`Damp and heaviness`,`The inner leg`],pathway:`Starts at the big toe, runs along the inner foot and leg, enters the abdomen, and reaches the side of the chest. A great luo is described wrapping the ribs at SP21.`,note:`Yin earth channel. In this system the Spleen is a digestive idea, not only the anatomical organ.`},{id:`ht`,code:`HT`,name:`Heart`,hanzi:`手少阴心经`,pinyin:`Shou Shaoyin Xin Jing`,element:`fire`,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`si`,limb:`Hand Shaoyin`,clock:`11 a.m.–1 p.m.`,organ:`Heart`,classicalCount:9,themes:[`Shen, the spirit in classical language`,`Sleep and restlessness`,`The tongue and speech in five-element notes`,`The inner little-finger side of the arm`],pathway:`From the heart region it is drawn to the armpit, down the inner arm, and to the little finger. It is a short channel with a long symbolic role.`,note:`Yin fire channel, paired with Small Intestine. Pericardium is the other yin fire channel.`},{id:`si`,code:`SI`,name:`Small Intestine`,hanzi:`手太阳小肠经`,pinyin:`Shou Taiyang Xiaochang Jing`,element:`fire`,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`ht`,limb:`Hand Taiyang`,clock:`1–3 p.m.`,organ:`Small Intestine`,classicalCount:19,themes:[`Shoulder blade and the back of the arm`,`Ear and jaw`,`Sorting the clear from the turbid, as a metaphor`,`Opening the Du vessel at SI3`],pathway:`From the little finger it runs up the back of the arm, across the shoulder blade, up the neck, and to the ear and cheek.`,note:`Yang fire channel. SI3 is also taught as the opening point of the Du vessel.`},{id:`bl`,code:`BL`,name:`Bladder`,hanzi:`足太阳膀胱经`,pinyin:`Zu Taiyang Pangguang Jing`,element:`water`,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`ki`,limb:`Foot Taiyang`,clock:`3–5 p.m.`,organ:`Bladder`,classicalCount:67,themes:[`The back and the spine`,`Back-shu points for the organs`,`Headache at the nape`,`The largest classical channel`],pathway:`Starts at the inner eye, climbs over the head, and descends the back in two lines on each side of the spine, then continues down the back of the leg to the little toe.`,note:`Yang water channel. Many organ shu points live here. This atlas includes the ones people ask for first; dozens of numbered points are still open to add.`},{id:`ki`,code:`KI`,name:`Kidney`,hanzi:`足少阴肾经`,pinyin:`Zu Shaoyin Shen Jing`,element:`water`,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`bl`,limb:`Foot Shaoyin`,clock:`5–7 p.m.`,organ:`Kidney`,classicalCount:27,themes:[`Essence and deep reserves, as teaching language`,`Low back, ears, and bones`,`Fear in the five-element set`,`The inner ankle and sole`],pathway:`Begins on the sole, circles the inner ankle, climbs the inner leg, and travels the abdomen and chest toward the collarbone.`,note:`Yin water channel, paired with Bladder. KI6 opens the Yin Qiao vessel.`},{id:`pc`,code:`PC`,name:`Pericardium`,alias:`Heart protector`,hanzi:`手厥阴心包经`,pinyin:`Shou Jueyin Xinbao Jing`,element:`fire`,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`sj`,limb:`Hand Jueyin`,clock:`7–9 p.m.`,organ:`Pericardium`,classicalCount:9,themes:[`Protecting the heart in classical imagery`,`Chest tightness and nausea`,`Calm, paired with San Jiao`,`The middle of the inner arm`],pathway:`From the chest it descends the middle of the inner arm, between the tendons at the wrist, and ends at the middle finger.`,note:`Yin fire channel. PC6 is the point most people already know for nausea. It also opens the Yin Wei vessel.`},{id:`sj`,code:`SJ`,name:`San Jiao`,alias:`Triple Burner`,hanzi:`手少阳三焦经`,pinyin:`Shou Shaoyang Sanjiao Jing`,element:`fire`,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`pc`,limb:`Hand Shaoyang`,clock:`9–11 p.m.`,organ:`San Jiao`,classicalCount:23,themes:[`A functional idea of upper, middle, and lower burners`,`The side of the arm and the ear`,`Waterways, in classical metaphor`,`Opening the Yang Wei vessel`],pathway:`From the ring finger it runs up the back of the forearm, the shoulder, the neck, and around the ear to the outer eyebrow.`,note:`Yang fire channel. Also called Triple Burner or Triple Heater. It is not a single anatomical organ.`},{id:`gb`,code:`GB`,name:`Gallbladder`,hanzi:`足少阳胆经`,pinyin:`Zu Shaoyang Dan Jing`,element:`wood`,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`lv`,limb:`Foot Shaoyang`,clock:`11 p.m.–1 a.m.`,organ:`Gallbladder`,classicalCount:44,themes:[`The side of the head and body`,`Decision and sinews, in teaching language`,`Neck and shoulder`,`Migraine-pattern headaches people look up`],pathway:`Zigzags around the side of the head, descends the neck and the side of the trunk, crosses the hip, and runs down the side of the leg to the fourth toe.`,note:`Yang wood channel, paired with Liver. GB41 opens the Dai vessel.`},{id:`lv`,code:`LV`,name:`Liver`,alias:`Sometimes written LR`,hanzi:`足厥阴肝经`,pinyin:`Zu Jueyin Gan Jing`,element:`wood`,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`gb`,limb:`Foot Jueyin`,clock:`1–3 a.m.`,organ:`Liver`,classicalCount:14,themes:[`Smooth flow of qi, a core teaching phrase`,`Eyes and sinews`,`Menstrual discomfort in traditional language`,`The inner leg and the ribs`],pathway:`From the big toe it climbs the top of the foot and the inner leg, circles the genitals in classical drawings, enters the lower abdomen, and spreads in the ribs toward the eye.`,note:`Yin wood channel. Some modern charts use LR instead of LV. The numbers are the same.`},{id:`ren`,code:`Ren`,name:`Ren`,alias:`Conception vessel · CV`,hanzi:`任脉`,pinyin:`Ren Mai`,element:null,polarity:`yin`,pairId:`du`,limb:`Anterior midline`,clock:null,organ:`Sea of yin`,classicalCount:24,themes:[`The front midline`,`Yin, fluids, and the uterus in classical language`,`Several front-mu points`,`Often called the Conception vessel`],pathway:`Rises on the front midline from the perineum, through the abdomen and chest, to the chin and the mouth. Search also finds these points as CV.`,note:`One of the eight extraordinary vessels, and the only yin vessel in that set with its own full point numbers in ordinary charts. LU7 is the opening point.`},{id:`du`,code:`Du`,name:`Du`,alias:`Governing vessel · GV`,hanzi:`督脉`,pinyin:`Du Mai`,element:null,polarity:`yang`,pairId:`ren`,limb:`Posterior midline`,clock:null,organ:`Sea of yang`,classicalCount:28,themes:[`The spine and the crown`,`Yang and the back`,`Clear-headedness in teaching language`,`Often called the Governing vessel`],pathway:`Rises on the back midline from the tailbone, up the spine, over the head, and down the face to the gum. Search also finds these points as GV.`,note:`Extraordinary vessel with its own point numbers. SI3 is the opening point. Treat the upper neck points with extra respect.`},{id:`extra`,code:`EX`,name:`Extra points`,hanzi:`经外奇穴`,pinyin:`Jingwai qixue`,element:null,polarity:null,pairId:null,limb:`Not on the 14 channels`,clock:null,organ:`Extra points`,classicalCount:6,themes:[`Well-known points outside the numbered channels`,`Clearly labeled so they are not mistaken for meridian points`],pathway:`These points do not belong to a primary channel. Each one sits near a channel but keeps its own name.`,note:`This atlas includes a short, famous set: Yintang, Anmian, Dingchuan, Taiyang, Sishencong, and Zigong. Many more extra points exist and can be added beside them.`}],C=S.map(e=>e.id);function w(e){return S.find(t=>t.id===e)}var T=[{id:`ma-huang`,pinyin:`Ma Huang`,hanzi:`麻黄`,english:`Ephedra`,latin:`Ephedra sinica`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`lu`,`bl`],summary:`Strongly releases the surface. A regulated plant, not a home stimulant.`,uses:`Classically the lead herb for a wind-cold pattern with no sweat, chills, and a tight surface. Also appears in old formulas for wheezing. Modern products that concentrate ephedrine have caused serious harm. The plant is restricted in many places.`,symptoms:[`immunity`,`breathing`],pairings:[{herbId:`gui-zhi`,idea:`Together they are the teaching pair for a cold surface that has not yet opened, the idea behind Ma Huang Tang. Study only.`},{herbId:`wu-wei-zi`,idea:`Paired when a text wants to open the lung and also hold a cough, so the dispersing herb is not left alone.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not for pregnancy, high blood pressure, heart disease, insomnia, or long-term use. Do not buy it as a weight-loss or sports product.`,tags:[`ephedra`,`wind cold`,`wheeze`]},{id:`gui-zhi`,pinyin:`Gui Zhi`,hanzi:`桂枝`,english:`Cinnamon twig`,latin:`Cinnamomum cassia (twig)`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[`tonify-yang`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`lu`,`bl`,`ht`],summary:`Warms the channels and helps a mild surface pattern.`,uses:`The twig, not the thick bark. Teaching use is to release the muscle layer, warm the limbs, and support a weak surface. It shows up with Bai Shao when a text wants to balance warming and softening.`,symptoms:[`immunity`,`tension`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`bai-shao`,idea:`Gui Zhi and Bai Shao are the pair that warms and also protects yin and the nutritive level, the skeleton of Gui Zhi Tang.`},{herbId:`ma-huang`,idea:`A stronger surface combination. See Ma Huang. Not a tea to invent.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Use care in pregnancy and with heavy menstrual bleeding. Warm herbs can feel agitating if there is already heat.`,tags:[`cinnamon twig`,`cassia`,`wind cold`]},{id:`bo-he`,pinyin:`Bo He`,hanzi:`薄荷`,english:`Field mint`,latin:`Mentha haplocalyx`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[`move-qi`],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`acrid`],channelIds:[`lu`,`lv`],summary:`Cool and aromatic. A light herb for wind-heat and a tight head.`,uses:`Used when a fresh pattern looks like wind-heat: sore throat, headache, and red eyes in textbook language. Also said to gently move liver qi because it is aromatic. The cooking note in schools is “add near the end,” which tells you the aroma is the point.`,symptoms:[`immunity`,`headache`,`stress`,`sinus`],pairings:[{herbId:`ju-hua`,idea:`Both rise to the head and eyes. A gentle classroom pair for wind-heat in the face.`},{herbId:`chai-hu`,idea:`Mint’s lightness is sometimes added when liver qi feels stuck and the head is tight.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Large amounts are traditionally avoided in nursing and in qi deficiency with lots of sweating. Peppermint tea is a different, milder plant use and still not a treatment.`,tags:[`mint`,`wind heat`,`sore throat`]},{id:`ju-hua`,pinyin:`Ju Hua`,hanzi:`菊花`,english:`Chrysanthemum flower`,latin:`Chrysanthemum morifolium`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[`clear-heat`],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`sweet`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`lu`,`lv`],summary:`Cools the head and is the flower students learn for the eyes.`,uses:`White chrysanthemum is the usual medicinal flower. Texts send it to headache, red or tired eyes, and a light wind-heat. It does not fix glaucoma, infection, or vision loss.`,symptoms:[`headache`,`immunity`,`stress`,`eyes`,`sinus`],pairings:[{herbId:`bo-he`,idea:`A light pair for the head when heat is at the surface.`},{herbId:`gou-qi-zi`,idea:`Flower and fruit of the eye lesson: one cools, one nourishes. A common tea idea, still not a dose.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing as medicine. People with chrysanthemum or ragweed allergy should be careful even with tea.`,tags:[`chrysanthemum`,`eyes`,`headache`]},{id:`ge-gen`,pinyin:`Ge Gen`,hanzi:`葛根`,english:`Kudzu root`,latin:`Pueraria lobata`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`sweet`,`acrid`],channelIds:[`sp`,`st`],summary:`Releases the muscle layer, especially a stiff neck.`,uses:`The textbook herb for a stiff nape at the start of a surface pattern, and for generating fluids when the pattern includes thirst. Also discussed for raising clear yang, which is why it sometimes sits near Sheng Ma in study notes.`,symptoms:[`tension`,`immunity`,`headache`],pairings:[{herbId:`gui-zhi`,idea:`Neck stiffness with a cold surface is the usual reason these two are written together.`},{herbId:`sheng-ma`,idea:`Both are said to lift clear yang. A raising pair, easy to overuse if there is already heat above.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy. Kudzu supplements sold for other purposes are not this tradition and not reviewed here.`,tags:[`kudzu`,`pueraria`,`stiff neck`]},{id:`fang-feng`,pinyin:`Fang Feng`,hanzi:`防风`,english:`Saposhnikovia root`,latin:`Saposhnikovia divaricata`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`bl`,`lv`,`sp`],summary:`A milder wind herb, used when the surface is not extremely closed.`,uses:`Name means “guard against wind.” Less harsh than Ma Huang. Texts use it for wandering aches, a mild chill, and some skin-wind patterns. It is a supporting herb more often than a star.`,symptoms:[`immunity`,`pain`,`tension`],pairings:[{herbId:`bai-zhi`,idea:`Two surface herbs: Fang Feng for the body, Bai Zhi when the face and forehead are the focus.`},{herbId:`ge-gen`,idea:`A milder wind pair: Ge Gen for the stiff nape, Fang Feng for a wandering surface ache.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Warm and drying if used a long time in a person who is already dry.`,tags:[`siler`,`saposhnikovia`,`wind`]},{id:`bai-zhi`,pinyin:`Bai Zhi`,hanzi:`白芷`,english:`Angelica dahurica root`,latin:`Angelica dahurica`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`],channelIds:[`lu`,`st`],summary:`Opens the nose and the yang brightness of the face.`,uses:`The usual herb in a textbook when the forehead, sinuses, or upper teeth are the region of a wind pattern. Aromatic and warm. It is not a decongestant you can safely stack with other drugs on your own.`,symptoms:[`headache`,`immunity`,`breathing`,`sinus`],pairings:[{herbId:`chuan-xiong`,idea:`A head-pain pair: Bai Zhi for the front, Chuan Xiong moving the qi and blood of the head.`},{herbId:`bo-he`,idea:`Warm and cool together when a stuffy head is not clearly one temperature.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Traditionally used carefully in pregnancy because it is acrid and moving.`,tags:[`dahurican angelica`,`sinus`,`forehead`]},{id:`sheng-jiang`,pinyin:`Sheng Jiang`,hanzi:`生姜`,english:`Fresh ginger`,latin:`Zingiber officinale (fresh rhizome)`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[`transform-phlegm`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`],channelIds:[`lu`,`sp`,`st`],summary:`Warms the middle and is the kitchen herb for mild nausea.`,uses:`Fresh ginger, not dried. Texts use it to release a mild surface, warm the stomach, and stop vomiting in a cold pattern. Dried ginger (Gan Jiang) is hotter and is a different entry you can add later.`,symptoms:[`nausea`,`digestion`,`immunity`],pairings:[{herbId:`da-zao`,idea:`Ginger and jujube harmonize a formula and the middle. They show up together so a dispersing herb is less harsh.`},{herbId:`ban-xia`,idea:`The nausea pair: Ban Xia transforms phlegm, ginger warms and also reduces Ban Xia’s harshness in classical preparation notes.`}],cautions:`Food use is ordinary. Medicinal amounts are still not for self-dosing. Care with reflux that is already hot, and with blood-thinning drugs if intake is large.`,tags:[`ginger`,`nausea`,`vomiting`]},{id:`sheng-ma`,pinyin:`Sheng Ma`,hanzi:`升麻`,english:`Cimicifuga rhizome`,latin:`Cimicifuga foetida`,action:`release-exterior`,also:[`tonify-qi`],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`acrid`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`li`,`lu`,`sp`,`st`],summary:`Raises yang and vents a heat rash in textbook language.`,uses:`Two classroom jobs: helping a collapsed middle “lift,” and venting measles-like surface heat in historical pediatrics. The raising idea is why it is mentioned with Du20 and prolapse patterns. It is not a hormone strategy.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`immunity`],pairings:[{herbId:`huang-qi`,idea:`Huang Qi tonifies, Sheng Ma lifts. The pair is the teaching skeleton of formulas that raise sinking qi.`},{herbId:`ge-gen`,idea:`Both can lift and release. Used together when the head and the neck are part of the pattern.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy. People sometimes confuse it with black cohosh supplements; species and dose are not interchangeable, and those products have their own risks.`,tags:[`cimicifuga`,`raise yang`]},{id:`huang-qin`,pinyin:`Huang Qin`,hanzi:`黄芩`,english:`Scutellaria root`,latin:`Scutellaria baicalensis`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[`drain-damp`],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`bitter`],channelIds:[`lu`,`li`,`st`,`gb`],summary:`Clears heat and dries damp, especially in the upper burner and the gut.`,uses:`One of the three yellows. Texts use it for lung heat, stomach heat, and restless fetus patterns — the last only inside a prescribed formula, never as a self-chosen herb in pregnancy.`,symptoms:[`immunity`,`digestion`,`breathing`],pairings:[{herbId:`huang-lian`,idea:`Two cold bitters. Qin is often aimed higher, Lian at the middle. Together they are very cooling.`},{herbId:`chai-hu`,idea:`The shaoyang pair students memorize from Xiao Chai Hu Tang. Harmonizing, not a home mix.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cold and bitter herbs injure the spleen if taken casually. Pregnancy use belongs only to a licensed prescriber.`,tags:[`scutellaria`,`baicalin`,`bitter`]},{id:`huang-lian`,pinyin:`Huang Lian`,hanzi:`黄连`,english:`Coptis rhizome`,latin:`Coptis chinensis`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`bitter`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`,`st`,`li`],summary:`Very bitter, very cold. Clears heat in the heart and stomach in textbooks.`,uses:`Used for a pattern of damp-heat or fire in the middle: bitter taste, irritability, and hot diarrhea as classical pictures. Also for heart-fire restlessness. It is one of the strongest common bitters.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`stress`],pairings:[{herbId:`huang-qin`,idea:`Upper and middle heat, a draining pair.`},{herbId:`ban-xia`,idea:`In some formulas the cold of Coptis is set against the warmth of Ban Xia for a mixed stomach pattern.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not for long-term use. Very cold. Can be hard on digestion. Species are over-harvested; quality and identity matter.`,tags:[`coptis`,`berberine`,`bitter`]},{id:`huang-bai`,pinyin:`Huang Bai`,hanzi:`黄柏`,english:`Phellodendron bark`,latin:`Phellodendron chinense`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[`drain-damp`],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`bitter`],channelIds:[`ki`,`bl`],summary:`Clears damp-heat in the lower body in textbook language.`,uses:`The third yellow. Aimed at the lower burner: hot urinary patterns, damp-heat in the legs, and some skin pictures. Drain the picture, do not self-treat an infection.`,symptoms:[`womens`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`zhi-mu`,idea:`A yin-heat pair from the lower-burner lessons: bark drains, Zhi Mu also moistens.`},{herbId:`ze-xie`,idea:`Both address damp in the lower body, one more by heat, one more by water.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cold and bitter. Not a urinary-tract treatment. Care in pregnancy.`,tags:[`phellodendron`,`amur cork`,`damp heat`]},{id:`zhi-zi`,pinyin:`Zhi Zi`,hanzi:`栀子`,english:`Gardenia fruit`,latin:`Gardenia jasminoides`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`bitter`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lu`,`st`,`lv`,`sj`],summary:`Drains heat and irritability through the three burners, in the usual phrase.`,uses:`Used when heat is restless: irritability, a hot chest, and jaundice patterns in classical internal medicine. It does not treat hepatitis or mood disorders.`,symptoms:[`stress`,`digestion`],pairings:[{herbId:`dan-shen`,idea:`Sometimes set together when heat and blood stasis are both in the teaching case.`},{herbId:`huang-qin`,idea:`Two bitters for a hotter, damper pattern.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cold. Traditional notes say it can loosen the stools. Not a home remedy for jaundice.`,tags:[`gardenia`,`irritability`]},{id:`lian-qiao`,pinyin:`Lian Qiao`,hanzi:`连翘`,english:`Forsythia fruit`,latin:`Forsythia suspensa`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`bitter`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`,`gb`],summary:`Clears heat-toxin and is usually written with honeysuckle.`,uses:`A light, cooling fruit for sore throat, early hot sores, and wind-heat in the upper body. The “toxin” word is traditional. It is not an antibiotic.`,symptoms:[`immunity`],pairings:[{herbId:`jin-yin-hua`,idea:`The classic heat-toxin pair, the heart of Yin Qiao formulas. Study the idea; do not compound it.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cool herbs are a poor match for a cold, pale, no-thirst pattern.`,tags:[`forsythia`,`sore throat`]},{id:`jin-yin-hua`,pinyin:`Jin Yin Hua`,hanzi:`金银花`,english:`Honeysuckle flower`,latin:`Lonicera japonica`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`lu`,`st`,`li`],summary:`A sweet cold flower for heat in the upper body.`,uses:`Paired constantly with Forsythia. Texts use it at the start of a hot surface pattern and for hot swellings. Sweet makes it slightly less harsh than the bitter yellows, not safer to invent a dose.`,symptoms:[`immunity`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`lian-qiao`,idea:`Flower and capsule, the standard heat-clearing introduction.`},{herbId:`bo-he`,idea:`Adds a light aromatic vent so heat is not only slammed downward.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not for a chill-without-heat pattern. Quality problems with wild honeysuckle products are common.`,tags:[`honeysuckle`,`lonicera`]},{id:`shi-gao`,pinyin:`Shi Gao`,hanzi:`石膏`,english:`Gypsum`,latin:`Gypsum fibrosum`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`sweet`,`acrid`],channelIds:[`lu`,`st`],summary:`A mineral that texts use for big heat and big thirst.`,uses:`Cooked gypsum is the textbook mineral for a yang-ming heat pattern: high fever, sweat, thirst, and a flooding pulse, in the old descriptions. That picture overlaps real emergencies. It is not a home fever powder.`,symptoms:[`immunity`],pairings:[{herbId:`zhi-mu`,idea:`The pair inside Bai Hu Tang: mineral clears, Zhi Mu protects fluids. Hospital-level patterns, not a tea.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Very cold. Do not use it to sit out a high fever. Mineral identity and preparation matter.`,tags:[`gypsum`,`fever`]},{id:`zhi-mu`,pinyin:`Zhi Mu`,hanzi:`知母`,english:`Anemarrhena rhizome`,latin:`Anemarrhena asphodeloides`,action:`clear-heat`,also:[`tonify-yin`],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`bitter`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`lu`,`st`,`ki`],summary:`Clears heat and also moistens, unlike the drying bitters.`,uses:`Used for lung and stomach heat with dryness, and for deficiency heat — afternoon warmth, nights, thirst — in yin-deficient teaching cases. One of the few clear-heat herbs that is also said to nourish.`,symptoms:[`breathing`,`stress`,`immunity`],pairings:[{herbId:`shi-gao`,idea:`Big heat with thirst.`},{herbId:`huang-bai`,idea:`Lower-burner yin heat, a standard pair.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cold. Loose stool is the usual caution if the spleen is weak.`,tags:[`anemarrhena`,`yin heat`]},{id:`fu-ling`,pinyin:`Fu Ling`,hanzi:`茯苓`,english:`Poria`,latin:`Poria cocos`,action:`drain-damp`,also:[`calm-shen`],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`sweet`,`bland`],channelIds:[`ht`,`sp`,`lu`,`ki`],summary:`A mild fungus that leaches damp and quiets the heart in textbooks.`,uses:`The most common damp herb in formulas because it is neutral and gentle. Texts say it strengthens the spleen, promotes urination, and calms the spirit when damp is clouding. It is food-like in some traditions and still not a dose to invent.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`stress`,`sleep`,`urinary`],pairings:[{herbId:`bai-zhu`,idea:`The spleen pair: Bai Zhu dries, Fu Ling drains. Together they are half of Si Jun Zi Tang’s idea.`},{herbId:`suan-zao-ren`,idea:`When sleep trouble is framed as restless spirit with a weak middle.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing as medicine. Care with edema that has a heart or kidney cause — that needs a clinician, not a fungus.`,tags:[`poria`,`hoelen`,`damp`]},{id:`ze-xie`,pinyin:`Ze Xie`,hanzi:`泽泻`,english:`Alisma rhizome`,latin:`Alisma plantago-aquatica`,action:`drain-damp`,also:[],nature:`cold`,flavors:[`sweet`,`bland`],channelIds:[`ki`,`bl`],summary:`Drains damp from the lower body and is said to settle ministerial fire.`,uses:`A water-plant rhizome used for urinary damp and for a heavy, wet lower body. Colder than Fu Ling. Often sits in kidney-yin formulas so the rich tonics do not feel cloying.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`womens`,`urinary`],pairings:[{herbId:`fu-ling`,idea:`Two bland drainers. Fu Ling is broader, Ze Xie more downward.`},{herbId:`shu-di`,idea:`In Liu Wei Di Huang Wan the rich root is paired with Ze Xie so the formula still moves water.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not a diuretic to combine with water pills. Care in pregnancy.`,tags:[`alisma`,`water plantain`]},{id:`yi-yi-ren`,pinyin:`Yi Yi Ren`,hanzi:`薏苡仁`,english:`Coix seed`,latin:`Coix lacryma-jobi`,action:`drain-damp`,also:[],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`sweet`,`bland`],channelIds:[`sp`,`st`,`lu`],summary:`A grain-like seed that drains damp and eases a heavy body.`,uses:`Job’s tears. Texts use it for damp obstructing the spleen, a heavy limbs feeling, and some damp skin or joint pictures. Eaten as food in some cuisines at food doses, which is not the same as a concentrated extract.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`pain`,`tension`],pairings:[{herbId:`fu-ling`,idea:`A gentle damp-draining pair, often the first one students taste in congee stories.`},{herbId:`bai-zhu`,idea:`Supports the spleen while the seed leaches damp.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing as a concentrated herb. Traditional caution in pregnancy. Food amounts in a meal are a separate, cultural use.`,tags:[`coix`,`job's tears`,`yi yi ren`]},{id:`ban-xia`,pinyin:`Ban Xia`,hanzi:`半夏`,english:`Pinellia rhizome`,latin:`Pinellia ternata`,action:`transform-phlegm`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`],channelIds:[`sp`,`st`,`lu`],summary:`The main phlegm herb. Raw pinellia is toxic. Only prepared herb belongs in a formula.`,uses:`Transforms phlegm, directs rebellious stomach qi downward, and is the nausea herb in dozens of formulas. Preparation (ginger-processed and others) is part of the medicine. Raw herb is caustic.`,symptoms:[`nausea`,`digestion`,`breathing`],pairings:[{herbId:`sheng-jiang`,idea:`Ginger is both a partner for nausea and part of how the tradition reduces Ban Xia’s harshness.`},{herbId:`chen-pi`,idea:`The phlegm-and-qi pair of Er Chen Tang: one transforms phlegm, one moves qi so phlegm does not sit.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Toxic if raw or poorly prepared. Care in pregnancy. Do not forage it.`,tags:[`pinellia`,`phlegm`,`nausea`,`toxic`]},{id:`chen-pi`,pinyin:`Chen Pi`,hanzi:`陈皮`,english:`Aged tangerine peel`,latin:`Citrus reticulata (pericarp)`,action:`move-qi`,also:[`transform-phlegm`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`sp`,`lu`],summary:`Moves qi in the middle and dries damp phlegm.`,uses:`Aged citrus peel. The longer-aged peel is the medicinal ideal. Texts use it for fullness, belching, and a stomach that feels stuck, and as the qi mover inside phlegm formulas.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`nausea`,`breathing`],pairings:[{herbId:`ban-xia`,idea:`Qi and phlegm. The pair assumes phlegm stays put when qi does not move.`},{herbId:`bai-zhu`,idea:`When the spleen is tired and the belly is both weak and full.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing in concentrated form. Dried peel in food is milder. Acrid and drying if you are already dry.`,tags:[`tangerine peel`,`citrus`,`fullness`]},{id:`hou-po`,pinyin:`Hou Po`,hanzi:`厚朴`,english:`Magnolia bark`,latin:`Magnolia officinalis`,action:`move-qi`,also:[`transform-phlegm`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`bitter`,`acrid`],channelIds:[`li`,`lu`,`sp`,`st`],summary:`Moves qi and dries damp in a stuck, full abdomen.`,uses:`A downward, aromatic bark for distension, especially when damp and food sit together. Also used for wheezing framed as phlegm above. Not a laxative to experiment with.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`breathing`],pairings:[{herbId:`chen-pi`,idea:`Two qi movers. Hou Po is heavier and more for distension.`},{herbId:`mu-xiang`,idea:`When the abdomen is both full and painful in a qi-stuck teaching case.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy. Drying. Magnolia bark products vary in species.`,tags:[`magnolia bark`,`distension`]},{id:`xiang-fu`,pinyin:`Xiang Fu`,hanzi:`香附`,english:`Cyperus rhizome`,latin:`Cyperus rotundus`,action:`move-qi`,also:[],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`acrid`,`bitter`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`lv`,`sj`],summary:`The qi herb most often aimed at the liver and the menstrual cycle.`,uses:`Moves liver qi without being very hot or very cold, which is why it is everywhere in cycle formulas. Texts say it regulates menstruation because it moves qi, and qi leads blood. That is a theory, not a promise about pain or fertility.`,symptoms:[`womens`,`stress`,`digestion`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`chai-hu`,idea:`Two liver-qi herbs. Chai Hu lifts and vents, Xiang Fu is more about the lower abdomen and the cycle.`},{herbId:`dang-gui`,idea:`Qi and blood together, a standard women’s-health study pair.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy because it moves. Not for heavy bleeding you have not had checked.`,tags:[`cyperus`,`nut grass`,`period`]},{id:`chai-hu`,pinyin:`Chai Hu`,hanzi:`柴胡`,english:`Bupleurum root`,latin:`Bupleurum chinense`,action:`move-qi`,also:[`release-exterior`],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`acrid`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`lv`,`gb`,`pc`,`sj`],summary:`Soothes liver qi and is the shaoyang herb.`,uses:`Two jobs students must not blur: venting a half-exterior pattern (alternating feelings, rib fullness) and spreading liver qi when emotion and the ribs are the story. It also raises yang in some formulas. Overuse is a known way formulas become too drying and lifting.`,symptoms:[`stress`,`digestion`,`headache`,`immunity`],pairings:[{herbId:`bai-shao`,idea:`The essential liver pair: Chai Hu moves, Bai Shao softens and protects yin.`},{herbId:`huang-qin`,idea:`The shaoyang pair. Harmonizing formulas, not a DIY decoction.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care with liver disease, interstitial lung disease reports in some formulas, and pregnancy. Raising herbs can worsen a headache that is already from excess above.`,tags:[`bupleurum`,`liver qi`,`shaoyang`]},{id:`mu-xiang`,pinyin:`Mu Xiang`,hanzi:`木香`,english:`Aucklandia root`,latin:`Aucklandia lappa`,action:`move-qi`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`sp`,`st`,`li`,`gb`],summary:`Aromatic, moves qi, and is aimed at intestinal pain and tenesmus in texts.`,uses:`Used when the belly is stuck and painful, especially around the bowels. Some pharmacopoeias now prefer related species because of conservation. The teaching idea is the same: wake the qi of the middle.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`bai-zhu`,idea:`Tonify and move, so a spleen tonic does not sit like a wet sponge.`},{herbId:`chen-pi`,idea:`Two aromatics for fullness. Mu Xiang is more for pain.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy. Some species are endangered; responsible sources matter. Not for acute abdominal emergencies.`,tags:[`costus`,`aucklandia`,`saussurea`]},{id:`chuan-xiong`,pinyin:`Chuan Xiong`,hanzi:`川芎`,english:`Szechuan lovage`,latin:`Ligusticum chuanxiong`,action:`move-blood`,also:[`move-qi`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`],channelIds:[`lv`,`gb`,`pc`],summary:`Moves qi and blood and is the head herb in many pain formulas.`,uses:`Acrid and moving, said to go to the head. Textbook uses include headache, and cycle pain framed as blood stasis. It is drying and ascending. Not every headache wants that.`,symptoms:[`headache`,`pain`,`womens`],pairings:[{herbId:`dang-gui`,idea:`A blood pair inside Si Wu Tang’s family: Dang Gui nourishes, Chuan Xiong moves.`},{herbId:`bai-zhi`,idea:`Headache pair for the yang-ming face and the shaoyang side.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Traditionally avoided in pregnancy and with heavy bleeding because it moves blood strongly. Can worsen a yin-deficient headache.`,tags:[`ligusticum`,`cnidium`,`headache`]},{id:`dan-shen`,pinyin:`Dan Shen`,hanzi:`丹参`,english:`Salvia root`,latin:`Salvia miltiorrhiza`,action:`move-blood`,also:[`clear-heat`],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`bitter`],channelIds:[`ht`,`pc`,`lv`],summary:`Moves blood and cools, with a special relationship to the heart in texts.`,uses:`Red sage root. Used for chest patterns, restlessness, and blood stasis with heat. Modern research interest in the heart does not make a root a cardiac drug. It interacts with real medicines.`,symptoms:[`stress`,`pain`,`sleep`,`palpitations`],pairings:[{herbId:`suan-zao-ren`,idea:`One moves and cools, one nourishes and calms. A heart-spirit study pair.`},{herbId:`hong-hua`,idea:`Two blood movers. Dan Shen is cooler.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Do not combine on your own with blood thinners, or before surgery. Care in pregnancy. Not a substitute for chest-pain care.`,tags:[`salvia`,`red sage`,`dan shen`]},{id:`hong-hua`,pinyin:`Hong Hua`,hanzi:`红花`,english:`Safflower`,latin:`Carthamus tinctorius`,action:`move-blood`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`],summary:`A strong blood mover. Small amounts in formulas, not a casual tea.`,uses:`Used for blood stasis: fixed pain, and cycle pain with clots in the traditional picture. Saffron is a different plant. Do not swap them.`,symptoms:[`pain`,`womens`],pairings:[{herbId:`tao-ren`,idea:`Seed and flower, the blood-stasis pair of Tao Hong Si Wu and many relatives.`},{herbId:`chuan-xiong`,idea:`Both move. Together they are distinctly not for pregnancy self-care.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Contraindicated in pregnancy in ordinary teaching. Not with heavy bleeding or blood thinners on your own.`,tags:[`safflower`,`carthamus`,`stasis`]},{id:`tao-ren`,pinyin:`Tao Ren`,hanzi:`桃仁`,english:`Peach kernel`,latin:`Prunus persica (seed)`,action:`move-blood`,also:[],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`bitter`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`ht`,`li`,`lv`],summary:`Breaks blood stasis and also moistens the intestines in textbooks.`,uses:`The kernel inside the peach pit, prepared. Used for fixed pain from stasis and for dry stool when stasis is part of the story. The raw pit is not a snack; preparation and dose are the medicine.`,symptoms:[`pain`,`womens`,`digestion`],pairings:[{herbId:`hong-hua`,idea:`The standard stasis pair.`},{herbId:`dang-gui`,idea:`Move and nourish, so blood is not only broken.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Pregnancy contraindication in standard texts. Bitter kernels can be toxic if misused. Not a food dose.`,tags:[`peach kernel`,`stasis`]},{id:`yan-hu-suo`,pinyin:`Yan Hu Suo`,hanzi:`延胡索`,english:`Corydalis rhizome`,latin:`Corydalis yanhusuo`,action:`move-blood`,also:[`move-qi`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`,`st`],summary:`The pain herb. Moves qi and blood. Not a home analgesic.`,uses:`Texts say it moves blood and qi in the chest and abdomen and is used for many kinds of pain. It contains alkaloids with real pharmacology. That is a reason for caution, not a reason to extract it in a kitchen.`,symptoms:[`pain`,`womens`,`digestion`],pairings:[{herbId:`chuan-xiong`,idea:`Both move pain. Corydalis is more specifically the “stop pain” herb in school.`},{herbId:`xiang-fu`,idea:`Qi constraint with pain, especially cycle pain in teaching cases.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy, with liver disease, and with other sedating or pain drugs. Not for long-term unsupervised use.`,tags:[`corydalis`,`pain`]},{id:`ren-shen`,pinyin:`Ren Shen`,hanzi:`人参`,english:`Ginseng root`,latin:`Panax ginseng`,action:`tonify-qi`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sweet`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`sp`,`lu`,`ht`],summary:`Strongly tonifies source qi. Easy to misuse, and not a stimulant.`,uses:`Asian ginseng. The textbook herb for collapse of qi and for a deep, empty tiredness with a weak appetite. American ginseng (Xi Yang Shen) is cooler and is a different herb. Energy-drink ginseng is not this dose or this diagnosis.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`digestion`,`memory`],pairings:[{herbId:`huang-qi`,idea:`Two qi tonics. Ren Shen is more about source qi, Huang Qi more about the surface and lifting.`},{herbId:`bai-zhu`,idea:`With Fu Ling and Gan Cao this is the four-gentlemen idea: rebuild the middle.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Can raise blood pressure and disturb sleep. Care with blood thinners, diabetes drugs, and stimulants. Not for a hot, excess pattern. Quality and species fraud are common.`,tags:[`ginseng`,`panax`,`fatigue`]},{id:`huang-qi`,pinyin:`Huang Qi`,hanzi:`黄芪`,english:`Astragalus root`,latin:`Astragalus membranaceus`,action:`tonify-qi`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`sp`,`lu`],summary:`Tonifies qi and is said to firm the surface.`,uses:`The common qi tonic for tiredness, a weak appetite, and a surface that catches every cold, in the traditional story. Also used to lift sinking qi and to support healing of slow wounds in classical surgery notes. It does not “boost immunity” as a slogan.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`immunity`,`digestion`],pairings:[{herbId:`dang-gui`,idea:`Qi and blood, the pair of Dang Gui Bu Xue Tang. Astragalus is used in a larger amount in that lesson.`},{herbId:`bai-zhu`,idea:`Two spleen qi tonics, one more lifting, one more drying of damp.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Traditionally held back when there is an acute exterior excess or significant heat. Care with autoimmune disease and immunosuppressant drugs — talk to a clinician rather than guessing.`,tags:[`astragalus`,`huang qi`,`fatigue`]},{id:`bai-zhu`,pinyin:`Bai Zhu`,hanzi:`白术`,english:`White atractylodes`,latin:`Atractylodes macrocephala`,action:`tonify-qi`,also:[`drain-damp`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`bitter`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`sp`,`st`],summary:`Strengthens the spleen and dries damp.`,uses:`The spleen’s representative tonic. Used for poor appetite, loose stool, and a heavy, damp middle. Different from Cang Zhu, which is more drying and less tonic. Do not swap the names.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`energy`],pairings:[{herbId:`fu-ling`,idea:`Dry and drain. The spleen-damp pair.`},{herbId:`huang-qi`,idea:`Rebuild qi when tiredness and a soft stool go together.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Drying if yin is already thin. Care in pregnancy only means “ask,” not “avoid forever”; prescribers use it, self-experimenters should not.`,tags:[`atractylodes`,`spleen`,`damp`]},{id:`gan-cao`,pinyin:`Gan Cao`,hanzi:`甘草`,english:`Licorice root`,latin:`Glycyrrhiza uralensis`,action:`tonify-qi`,also:[],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lu`,`sp`,`st`],summary:`Harmonizes formulas. Also the herb most likely to cause trouble if eaten daily in quantity.`,uses:`Sweet, said to tonify the spleen, moisten the lung, and moderate spasms and the harshness of other herbs. Raw and honey-fried are different preparations. It enters almost every classical formula in a small amount for a reason.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`energy`,`breathing`],pairings:[{herbId:`bai-shao`,idea:`A pair for cramping pain: sweetness and sourness, the idea of moderating a spasm.`},{herbId:`da-zao`,idea:`Two sweeteners that harmonize. Easy to overdo.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing or for daily licorice candy as medicine. Can raise blood pressure, lower potassium, and interact with many drugs. Care in pregnancy and with heart or kidney disease. This is the important caution.`,tags:[`licorice`,`glycyrrhiza`,`harmonize`]},{id:`dang-gui`,pinyin:`Dang Gui`,hanzi:`当归`,english:`Chinese angelica root`,latin:`Angelica sinensis`,action:`tonify-blood`,also:[`move-blood`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sweet`,`acrid`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`,`sp`],summary:`Nourishes and gently moves blood. The women’s-health herb everyone has heard of.`,uses:`Used for a pale, dry, blood-deficient picture and for cycle discomfort when blood needs both building and moving. Different angelicas (Bai Zhi, American angelica) are not substitutes. It is not a hormone.`,symptoms:[`womens`,`pain`,`energy`],pairings:[{herbId:`huang-qi`,idea:`Blood and qi. The classic ratio lesson.`},{herbId:`bai-shao`,idea:`Two blood herbs: Dang Gui moves a little, Bai Shao softens and holds yin.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy, with bleeding, and with blood thinners. Can upset a hot, excess pattern. Photosensitivity and allergy are possible.`,tags:[`angelica sinensis`,`dong quai`,`blood`]},{id:`shu-di`,pinyin:`Shu Di Huang`,hanzi:`熟地黄`,english:`Prepared rehmannia`,latin:`Rehmannia glutinosa (prepared root)`,action:`tonify-blood`,also:[`tonify-yin`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`ki`,`lv`],summary:`The heavy blood and yin tonic. Prepared root, not the raw one.`,uses:`Cooked in wine in the traditional process. Used for blood deficiency and for kidney and liver yin: low back, nights, dryness, and a thin picture in the old language. Sheng Di Huang, the raw root, is cooler and is a different herb to add later.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`womens`,`sleep`],pairings:[{herbId:`shan-zhu-yu`,idea:`Two of the three tonics in Liu Wei Di Huang Wan, with Chinese yam not yet in this atlas.`},{herbId:`dang-gui`,idea:`A rich blood pair. Cloying if the digestion is weak, which is why Chen Pi or Sha Ren often appear in real formulas.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Very cloying. Poor choice if there is damp, poor appetite, or loose stool. Care in pregnancy means ask a prescriber.`,tags:[`rehmannia`,`shu di`,`yin`]},{id:`bai-shao`,pinyin:`Bai Shao`,hanzi:`白芍`,english:`White peony root`,latin:`Paeonia lactiflora`,action:`tonify-blood`,also:[],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`bitter`,`sour`],channelIds:[`lv`,`sp`],summary:`Softens the liver, nourishes blood, and eases cramping.`,uses:`The peony students pair with Chai Hu so liver qi is not moved harshly. Also used for abdominal cramps and for cycle pain in a blood-deficient picture. Red peony (Chi Shao) moves and cools more; it is not the same.`,symptoms:[`stress`,`womens`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`chai-hu`,idea:`Move and soften. The liver-qi pair.`},{herbId:`gan-cao`,idea:`Sour and sweet for cramps, the Shao Yao Gan Cao lesson.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy and with diarrhea from deficiency cold. Do not confuse with peony supplements sold for hormones.`,tags:[`peony`,`bai shao`,`cramp`]},{id:`gou-qi-zi`,pinyin:`Gou Qi Zi`,hanzi:`枸杞子`,english:`Goji berry`,latin:`Lycium barbarum`,action:`tonify-yin`,also:[`tonify-blood`],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`ki`,`lv`,`lu`],summary:`A food-like fruit for liver and kidney yin, especially the eyes in texts.`,uses:`Wolfberry. Milder than Shu Di. Used for a dry, tired picture, low back, and blurred vision in the traditional eye lesson. The snack-food version is not a clinical dose, and a clinical dose is not a snack plan.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`stress`,`headache`,`memory`,`eyes`],pairings:[{herbId:`ju-hua`,idea:`Nourish and cool the eyes. A famous tea pair, still not treatment for eye disease.`},{herbId:`shu-di`,idea:`Both nourish liver and kidney. Gou Qi is lighter.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing as medicine. Care with blood thinners and blood-pressure drugs. Traditional note: not ideal if there is a lot of damp or loose stool.`,tags:[`goji`,`lycium`,`wolfberry`,`eyes`]},{id:`mai-men-dong`,pinyin:`Mai Men Dong`,hanzi:`麦门冬`,english:`Ophiopogon tuber`,latin:`Ophiopogon japonicus`,action:`tonify-yin`,also:[],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`sweet`,`bitter`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lu`,`st`],summary:`Moistens the lung and stomach and quiets irritability from dryness.`,uses:`A yin tonic for dry cough, thirst, and a restless feeling when fluids are thin. Different from Tian Men Dong, which is colder and aimed more at the kidney. Add that one later if you want the pair.`,symptoms:[`breathing`,`stress`,`sleep`],pairings:[{herbId:`wu-wei-zi`,idea:`Moisten and astringe the lung. A cough lesson, not a recipe.`},{herbId:`zhi-mu`,idea:`Both moisten while clearing heat. Zhi Mu is colder and aimed more at deficiency heat.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cloying and cool. A poor match for a wet cough or a very weak, loose digestion.`,tags:[`ophiopogon`,`mondo grass`,`dry cough`]},{id:`du-zhong`,pinyin:`Du Zhong`,hanzi:`杜仲`,english:`Eucommia bark`,latin:`Eucommia ulmoides`,action:`tonify-yang`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`ki`,`lv`],summary:`Warms and supports the low back and the kidneys in textbook language.`,uses:`The bark with white latex threads when snapped. Used for low-back weakness and a cold picture of kidney yang. Some textbooks also group it with erectile difficulty when the low back is weak and cold. That prenatal “calm the fetus” use you may see elsewhere is prescriber territory, not a tea. Not a treatment for ED.`,symptoms:[`pain`,`energy`,`womens`,`ed`,`low-back`],pairings:[{herbId:`shu-di`,idea:`Yang and yin support together so warming is not empty.`},{herbId:`rou-gui`,idea:`Two warmers. Rou Gui is hotter and more internal.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy except under a licensed prescriber. Not for a hot, excess low-back injury. Can affect blood pressure in some reports.`,tags:[`eucommia`,`low back`,`erectile`]},{id:`rou-gui`,pinyin:`Rou Gui`,hanzi:`肉桂`,english:`Cinnamon bark`,latin:`Cinnamomum cassia (bark)`,action:`tonify-yang`,also:[],nature:`hot`,flavors:[`acrid`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`ki`,`sp`,`ht`,`lv`],summary:`Hot bark that returns fire to the source, in the classical phrase.`,uses:`The thick bark, not Gui Zhi the twig. Used for a deep cold picture: cold limbs, cold lower abdomen, and a yang that is not holding. “Returning fire to the mingmen” is the phrase used when erectile difficulty is taught as kidney yang. Kitchen cinnamon is not the same dose, and neither is a treatment for ED.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`pain`,`digestion`,`ed`,`cold`],pairings:[{herbId:`shu-di`,idea:`Hot and rich. The bark is sometimes added so the heavy yin tonic still circulates.`},{herbId:`du-zhong`,idea:`Two kidney warmers, bark hotter than eucommia.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not in pregnancy in ordinary self-care. Not with bleeding. Heating. Cassia cinnamon in food quantities can also matter for the liver if it is a daily supplement — do not add capsules on a whim.`,tags:[`cinnamon bark`,`rou gui`,`yang`,`erectile`]},{id:`suan-zao-ren`,pinyin:`Suan Zao Ren`,hanzi:`酸枣仁`,english:`Spiny jujube seed`,latin:`Ziziphus jujuba var. spinosa (seed)`,action:`calm-shen`,also:[],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`sweet`,`sour`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`,`gb`],summary:`The seed students learn first for sleep.`,uses:`Nourishes heart yin and liver blood and calms the spirit, especially when someone wakes easily or cannot settle and the picture is dry rather than hot and phlegmy. Different from Da Zao, the fruit.`,symptoms:[`sleep`,`stress`,`worry`,`palpitations`],pairings:[{herbId:`fu-ling`,idea:`The pair inside the classic sour-jujube decoction lesson: one nourishes, one drains so the spirit is not clogged.`},{herbId:`yuan-zhi`,idea:`Two shen herbs. Yuan Zhi is more acrid and connecting; the seed is more nourishing.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy and with sedative drugs. A very sour, oily seed can bother a weak stomach. Not a treatment for severe insomnia or depression.`,tags:[`ziziphus seed`,`suan zao ren`,`sleep`]},{id:`yuan-zhi`,pinyin:`Yuan Zhi`,hanzi:`远志`,english:`Polygala root`,latin:`Polygala tenuifolia`,action:`calm-shen`,also:[`transform-phlegm`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`bitter`,`acrid`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lu`],summary:`Connects the heart and kidney, in the phrase, and transforms phlegm that clouds the spirit.`,uses:`A small, acrid root for forgetfulness, restlessness, and a chest full of phlegm in the traditional spirit lesson. People now look it up for brain fog. It is more moving than Suan Zao Ren. Preparation often includes licorice or honey to reduce irritation. Not a memory drug and not care for dementia.`,symptoms:[`stress`,`sleep`,`memory`,`worry`],pairings:[{herbId:`suan-zao-ren`,idea:`Nourish and connect. A common shen pair.`},{herbId:`fu-ling`,idea:`When the “fog” is framed as phlegm-damp around the heart.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Can irritate the stomach. Care in pregnancy, with gastritis, and with sedatives. Not a memory drug.`,tags:[`polygala`,`yuan zhi`,`shen`,`memory`,`brain`]},{id:`long-gu`,pinyin:`Long Gu`,hanzi:`龙骨`,english:`Dragon bone`,latin:`Fossilized bone (traditional)`,action:`calm-shen`,also:[`stabilize-bind`],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`sweet`,`astringent`],channelIds:[`ht`,`lv`,`ki`],summary:`A heavy mineral substance used to settle the spirit.`,uses:`Traditionally fossil bone, calcined or raw depending on the text. Heavy substances are said to anchor yang and calm fright, palpitations, and a floating feeling. Modern supply, ethics, and heavy-metal contamination are real issues. It is a historical category to understand, not a product to hunt.`,symptoms:[`stress`,`sleep`,`worry`,`palpitations`],pairings:[{herbId:`suan-zao-ren`,idea:`One anchors, one nourishes. A settling pair in spirit formulas.`},{herbId:`yuan-zhi`,idea:`One anchors, one connects. Oyster shell is the other classic heavy pair and is not in this atlas yet.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Do not buy unidentified bone powders. Contamination and endangered-species rules both matter. Care with constipation and in pregnancy.`,tags:[`dragon bone`,`fossil`,`anchor`]},{id:`tian-ma`,pinyin:`Tian Ma`,hanzi:`天麻`,english:`Gastrodia tuber`,latin:`Gastrodia elata`,action:`extinguish-wind`,also:[],nature:`neutral`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`lv`],summary:`Extinguishes internal wind. The dizziness and headache herb in school.`,uses:`A neutral tuber, unusual because it is an orchid symbiont. Texts use it for headache, dizziness, and numbness framed as liver wind. It does not treat stroke, vertigo disease, or seizures. Those are emergencies or specialist care.`,symptoms:[`headache`,`stress`,`tension`,`dizziness`],pairings:[{herbId:`gou-teng`,idea:`The wind pair. Tian Ma is neutral, Gou Teng is cooler and is added later in cooking notes.`},{herbId:`chuan-xiong`,idea:`When wind and blood stasis are both used to explain a stubborn head pain.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Care in pregnancy. Conservation and cultivation matter; wild orchid products are a problem. Not for sudden neurological symptoms.`,tags:[`gastrodia`,`wind`,`dizziness`]},{id:`gou-teng`,pinyin:`Gou Teng`,hanzi:`钩藤`,english:`Uncaria vine`,latin:`Uncaria rhynchophylla`,action:`extinguish-wind`,also:[`clear-heat`],nature:`cool`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`lv`,`pc`],summary:`Cools the liver and extinguishes wind. Hooks are the medicinal part.`,uses:`The hooked vine. Used for headache, irritability, and a rising feeling when liver yang is the teaching pattern. Also appears in older pediatric convulsion formulas, which is historical context, not a home response to a seizure.`,symptoms:[`headache`,`stress`,`dizziness`],pairings:[{herbId:`tian-ma`,idea:`Cool plus neutral. The standard internal-wind introduction.`},{herbId:`ju-hua`,idea:`Both clear the head. Chrysanthemum is lighter.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Cooking instruction in schools is short and late, which already tells you not to boil it like a root. Care in pregnancy and with blood-pressure drugs. Seizures are medical emergencies.`,tags:[`uncaria`,`gambir`,`liver yang`]},{id:`wu-wei-zi`,pinyin:`Wu Wei Zi`,hanzi:`五味子`,english:`Schisandra fruit`,latin:`Schisandra chinensis`,action:`stabilize-bind`,also:[`tonify-yin`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sour`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`ht`,`ki`,`lu`],summary:`Astringes the lung and is said to hold leaking qi.`,uses:`Five-flavor fruit. Used for a chronic cough that is dry and empty, for sweat that will not stop, and for a spirit that is scattered, in textbook language. The sour taste is the astringent idea. It does not treat asthma.`,symptoms:[`breathing`,`sleep`,`energy`],pairings:[{herbId:`mai-men-dong`,idea:`Moisten and hold. A lung-yin pair.`},{herbId:`ren-shen`,idea:`In some famous formulas the fruit holds what the ginseng rebuilds.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not when there is an acute exterior pattern you are trying to release, or a damp internal pile-up. Care in pregnancy, with reflux, and with liver disease. Can interact with drugs metabolized by the liver.`,tags:[`schisandra`,`five flavor`]},{id:`shan-zhu-yu`,pinyin:`Shan Zhu Yu`,hanzi:`山茱萸`,english:`Cornus fruit`,latin:`Cornus officinalis`,action:`stabilize-bind`,also:[`tonify-yin`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sour`],channelIds:[`lv`,`ki`],summary:`Astringes and supports liver and kidney yin.`,uses:`Dogwood fruit. A pillar of the six-ingredient rehmannia lesson. Used for a weak low back and a thin yin picture. “Holding essence” is sometimes discussed beside erectile difficulty and urinary leaking in the traditional sense. Astringent means it holds; it should not be used to trap an acute infection, and it is not a treatment for ED.`,symptoms:[`energy`,`womens`,`ed`],pairings:[{herbId:`shu-di`,idea:`The yin pair at the center of Liu Wei Di Huang Wan.`},{herbId:`wu-wei-zi`,idea:`Two sour holders. Easy to make a formula too binding.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not appropriate when damp-heat or an acute urinary infection is the real problem. Care in pregnancy.`,tags:[`cornus`,`dogwood`,`shan zhu yu`,`erectile`]},{id:`da-zao`,pinyin:`Da Zao`,hanzi:`大枣`,english:`Jujube fruit`,latin:`Ziziphus jujuba (fruit)`,action:`tonify-qi`,also:[`calm-shen`],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`sweet`],channelIds:[`sp`,`st`],summary:`A food fruit that harmonizes formulas and mildly supports the spleen.`,uses:`Chinese date. Used in small numbers in decoctions to strengthen the spleen, moderate harsh herbs, and calm the spirit a little. It is the gentlest qi tonic in this list and still not a reason to eat a bag as medicine.`,symptoms:[`digestion`,`energy`,`stress`],pairings:[{herbId:`sheng-jiang`,idea:`The harmonizing pair added to many surface formulas so they are less injuring.`},{herbId:`gan-cao`,idea:`Double sweetness. Harmonizing, and easy to make cloying.`}],cautions:`Food use is common. As a medicine, sweetness worsens damp, bloating, and some digestive weakness. Not a treatment. The seed (Suan Zao Ren) is a different herb.`,tags:[`jujube`,`chinese date`,`da zao`]},{id:`yin-yang-huo`,pinyin:`Yin Yang Huo`,hanzi:`淫羊藿`,english:`Epimedium`,latin:`Epimedium brevicornu or related species`,action:`tonify-yang`,also:[],nature:`warm`,flavors:[`acrid`,`sweet`],channelIds:[`ki`,`lv`],summary:`The kidney-yang herb students meet when textbooks discuss erectile difficulty.`,uses:`Sold as horny goat weed. Traditional language says it warms kidney yang and expels wind-damp, and it has a reputation for sexual function that shop labels exaggerate. Erectile difficulty has many causes, including heart disease, diabetes, and medicines. This is a name and a category, not a dose and not a treatment.`,symptoms:[`ed`,`energy`,`pain`],pairings:[{herbId:`du-zhong`,idea:`Two warm kidney herbs. Eucommia is aimed more at the low back.`},{herbId:`rou-gui`,idea:`Both warm yang. Rou Gui is hotter and more internal.`}],cautions:`Not for self-dosing. Not a treatment for ED. See a clinician if erections changed suddenly, or with chest pain, new medicines, or diabetes. Traditionally a poor match for yin deficiency with heat: nights, dryness, restlessness. Can affect blood pressure and interact with drugs. Not for pregnancy.`,tags:[`epimedium`,`horny goat weed`,`erectile`,`kidney yang`]}],E=`Educational only. This is not a personal dose and not medical advice. The range is a typical adult textbook amount for one day, usually as part of a formula. Ask a qualified herbalist or physician before you use any herb, especially if you are pregnant, nursing, taking medicine, or already ill.`,D={"ma-huang":{short:`A strong, restricted herb for a closed-up cold. Not a stimulant.`,what:`Traditionally used at the start of a cold when someone feels chilled, is not sweating, and the chest feels tight or wheezy. It is one of the strongest herbs for opening the surface. It is not a weight-loss product and not an energy herb.`,feel:`Warming, drying, and strongly opening. It pushes outward. Much harsher than ginger or cinnamon twig.`,who:`Students learning classical cold formulas. Not someone shopping for a workout or diet pill.`,buy:`Dried ephedra stems, if they are legal where you live. In the United States and many other countries, ephedra supplements are banned or tightly restricted. Do not buy anything sold as a fat burner.`,how:`In textbooks the dried stem is simmered about 20 minutes, almost always inside a full formula, not as a solo tea. Granules exist for licensed clinics. Skip it if you cannot confirm it is legal and appropriate.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 2–9 g of dried stem in one day, inside a formula. Not a home dose.`,look:`Dried ephedra stems — thin, jointed twigs, the crude herb, not a pill.`,alert:`Ma huang (ephedra) is restricted in the US and many countries. It is not a weight-loss or energy product. It can raise blood pressure and stress the heart. Do not use it on your own.`},"gui-zhi":{short:`Cinnamon twigs for a mild chill in the muscles.`,what:`These are cinnamon twigs, not the thick bark. Traditionally used when a mild cold sits in the muscles: a light chill, tight shoulders, or cold hands. Also used in formulas that warm the limbs.`,feel:`Warm, slightly sweet, and gently opening. Softer than ephedra, hotter than a kitchen sprinkle of cinnamon.`,who:`People reading about an early chill, cold hands, or mild aches that feel better with warmth.`,buy:`Dried cinnamon twigs, often cut into short sticks and labeled gui zhi. This is not cinnamon bark (rou gui) and not a spoon of cooking cinnamon as a measured substitute.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g in about 2–3 cups of water for 15–20 minutes. Shops also sell it as granules and in prepared teas. A formula is the usual traditional setting, not a strong daily drink.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried cinnamon twigs, the thin sticks sold as gui zhi.`},"bo-he":{short:`Mint for a hot, tight head or a scratchy throat.`,what:`Mint is used when a cold feels hot rather than chilly: sore throat, a tight forehead, or a stuffy hot nose. The smell is the working part, so it is added at the end of cooking or simply steeped.`,feel:`Cooling, light, and aromatic. It rises toward the head. It is not a heavy medicine.`,who:`People looking up a mild hot cold, a tight head, or the mint in a traditional tea.`,buy:`Dried mint leaves, ideally whole leaves that still smell fresh. Field mint is the classical species. Ordinary peppermint tea is a milder cousin, not the same measured herb.`,how:`Steep 3–6 g in hot water, covered, for 5–10 minutes. If it is in a simmered pot, add it in the last 5 minutes. Tea is the form most people actually use.`,range:`Textbook amount: 3–6 g, added near the end so the smell stays.`,look:`Dried mint leaves, the form used for tea.`},"ju-hua":{short:`Chrysanthemum flowers for a hot head and tired eyes.`,what:`Chrysanthemum is the flower people drink for a hot, tight head and for eyes that feel red, dry, or strained. It does not treat glaucoma, infection, or vision loss.`,feel:`Cool, lightly sweet, and a little bitter. It is aimed at the head and eyes.`,who:`People with screen-tired eyes or a mild heat headache who want the traditional flower tea.`,buy:`Whole dried chrysanthemum blossoms. White flowers are the usual medicinal kind. Yellow flowers are also sold. Skip dusty, scentless fragments.`,how:`Steep about 4.5–15 g in hot water, covered, for 5–10 minutes. The same flowers can be steeped once more. Tea bags are the everyday form.`,range:`Textbook amount: 4.5–15 g of dried flowers.`,look:`Dried chrysanthemum blossoms, the tea flower.`},"ge-gen":{short:`Kudzu root for a stiff neck at the start of a chill.`,what:`Kudzu root is the textbook herb for a stiff, achy neck at the start of a cold, and for thirst when the pattern also feels dry. It is a starchy root, often cooked in soup as well as medicine.`,feel:`Cool, sweet, and mildly spicy. In traditional language it relaxes the muscle layer of the neck and upper back.`,who:`People with a stiff nape after a chill, and students comparing it with cinnamon twig.`,buy:`Dried kudzu root slices. They look pale and starchy. Kudzu capsules sold for hangovers or other modern claims are a different product and not reviewed here.`,how:`Simmer 9–21 g for 20–30 minutes. Clinics often use granules inside a formula. The neck use is the one beginners should remember.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 9–21 g a day.`,look:`Dried kudzu root slices, pale and starchy.`},"fang-feng":{short:`A mild wind herb for a light chill and wandering aches.`,what:`The name means “guard against wind.” It is used for a light chill, aches that move around, and some traditional notes about drafty, itchy skin. It is usually a helper in a formula, not the star.`,feel:`Slightly warm, mildly spicy, and a little sweet. Gentler and less drying than ephedra.`,who:`People reading about a mild surface chill or wandering aches, not a high fever.`,buy:`Dried saposhnikovia root, sometimes labeled siler. Look for pale, aromatic slices from an herb shop.`,how:`Simmer 4.5–9 g for 15–20 minutes. Granules in a practitioner formula are the other common form.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 4.5–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried saposhnikovia root slices.`},"bai-zhi":{short:`A fragrant root for a stuffy forehead and sinus pressure.`,what:`Used when a cold or ache sits in the face: forehead pain, a blocked nose, or upper-tooth pressure in traditional language. It is warm and opening. It is not a drugstore decongestant to stack with other drugs.`,feel:`Warm, spicy, and strongly fragrant. It is aimed at the face, especially the forehead and nose.`,who:`People looking up a frontal headache or a stuffy face in traditional notes.`,buy:`Dried Angelica dahurica slices that still smell sweet and spicy. This is not dang gui, even though both are angelicas.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for about 15 minutes so the fragrance is not boiled away. Also sold as granules.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried Angelica dahurica root slices, creamy and fragrant.`,alert:`Traditionally used carefully in pregnancy because it is spicy and moving. Do not choose it on your own while pregnant.`},"sheng-jiang":{short:`Fresh ginger for a queasy stomach and a mild chill.`,what:`Fresh ginger is the kitchen herb for a queasy stomach and a mild chill. Texts use it to warm the stomach and ease vomiting that feels cold and watery. Dried ginger is a different, hotter herb.`,feel:`Warm and spicy. It wakes up digestion and the surface of the body. Food amounts are ordinary. Large medicinal amounts are a step up.`,who:`Anyone with ordinary nausea, a cold stomach, or a mild chill. This is the most familiar herb in the atlas.`,buy:`Fresh ginger root from a grocery store. For a traditional decoction, slice it fresh. Do not swap in dried ginger powder one-for-one.`,how:`Simmer a few slices, about 3–9 g, for 10 minutes, or steep them as tea. Ginger chews exist, but fresh slices are the real form. People with hot reflux may feel worse with a lot of it.`,range:`Textbook amount: 3–9 g of fresh ginger in one day.`,look:`Fresh ginger, cut so the yellow interior shows.`},"sheng-ma":{short:`A lifting root used in formulas, not a hormone herb.`,what:`Textbooks give this root two jobs: helping a sunken, drooping kind of fatigue lift, and venting a rash-like surface heat in older pediatric notes. It is not a hormone strategy.`,feel:`Cool, slightly spicy, and a little sweet. In traditional language it lifts.`,who:`Students of formulas that raise energy, often next to astragalus. Not a casual daily tea.`,buy:`Dried cimicifuga rhizome from an herb shop. Check the Latin name. Black cohosh capsules are a different species and not a swap.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for 15–20 minutes, almost always inside a formula rather than alone.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried cimicifuga rhizome, dark and knobby.`,alert:`Do not confuse this with black cohosh supplements. Pregnancy use belongs only with a licensed prescriber.`},"huang-qin":{short:`A cold, bitter root for heat in the chest or gut.`,what:`Chinese skullcap root is used when heat sits in the chest, stomach, or intestines: a bitter taste, irritability, or hot loose stools in textbook pictures. It is one of the three yellows. It is not the American skullcap sold for sleep.`,feel:`Cold, bitter, and drying. It clears heat. Taken casually, bitter cold herbs can upset digestion.`,who:`Students comparing heat-clearing roots. Not a pleasant wellness tea.`,buy:`Dried Scutellaria baicalensis slices with a yellow interior. Do not substitute American skullcap (Scutellaria lateriflora).`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for about 20 minutes. It is very bitter. Clinics usually give it as granules inside a formula. Pregnancy use belongs only to a prescriber.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried scutellaria root slices, yellow inside.`},"huang-lian":{short:`An extremely bitter root for strong stomach heat.`,what:`Coptis is one of the most bitter herbs in the book. Texts use a small amount for strong heat in the stomach or for restless heat: a burning loose stool, a bitter mouth, or irritability. A little goes a long way.`,feel:`Very cold and very bitter. It drains fire. It is not a daily tonic.`,who:`Students learning the strong bitters. Not someone looking for a pleasant tea.`,buy:`Dried coptis rhizome, small and yellow-brown. A berberine pill is one chemical from plants like this, not the whole herb.`,how:`Simmer 1.5–9 g, often toward the low end, for 15–20 minutes. It is usually a small piece of a formula, not a cup you drink for weeks.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 1.5–9 g a day. Many formulas stay near the bottom of that range.`,look:`Dried coptis rhizome, the very bitter yellow root.`},"huang-bai":{short:`Yellow bark for damp heat aimed at the lower body.`,what:`Phellodendron bark is the third yellow. Textbooks aim it lower than the other two: hot, urgent urinary pictures, damp sore joints, or lower-body heat. It does not treat an infection.`,feel:`Cold, bitter, and drying. It is said to drain downward.`,who:`Students of lower-body damp-heat patterns. It is too cold to take as a casual tonic.`,buy:`Bark pieces with a bright yellow inner face, labeled phellodendron or huang bai.`,how:`Simmer 3–12 g for about 20 minutes. Bitter. Usually combined with other herbs rather than taken alone.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–12 g a day.`,look:`Phellodendron bark, bright yellow on the inner side.`},"zhi-zi":{short:`Gardenia fruit for irritable, stuck heat.`,what:`Gardenia fruit is used when heat feels stuck and irritable: a restless hot chest, a bitter taste, or heat that shows up in the urine in old language. This is the dried fruit, not gardenia perfume or oil.`,feel:`Cold and bitter. It drains heat and is traditionally said to cool irritability.`,who:`Students of heat-clearing formulas, and people who have seen the dried fruit in an herb shop.`,buy:`Whole dried gardenia fruits, oblong and orange-red, or fruits that have been cracked for cooking. Essential oil is not the herb.`,how:`Simmer 6–9 g, often crushed, for 15–20 minutes. It is usually part of a formula. The fruit stains, so decoctions look yellow-orange.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried gardenia fruits, the orange-red pods.`},"lian-qiao":{short:`Forsythia fruit for the start of a hot sore throat.`,what:`Forsythia fruit is a cooling herb for the beginning of a hot cold: a sore throat, or the textbook picture of a heat rash. It is the classic partner of honeysuckle flower.`,feel:`Cool and slightly bitter. It vents heat outward rather than acting like a heavy antibiotic.`,who:`People looking up the first day of a hot cold, or the honeysuckle and forsythia pair.`,buy:`Dried forsythia fruits, small, tan to olive, often split open. Not an essential oil.`,how:`Simmer 6–15 g for about 15 minutes. Prepared “yin qiao” style products and granules are common in shops. A product on a shelf is still not a personal prescription.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried forsythia fruits, some split open.`},"jin-yin-hua":{short:`Honeysuckle flowers for a hot, scratchy throat.`,what:`Honeysuckle flower is the gentle cooling flower for a hot sore throat or the first day of a feverish cold in textbooks. It is often steeped, not boiled hard. It does not replace care for a serious infection.`,feel:`Cold and sweet. Light. It clears heat at the surface.`,who:`People who want the traditional flower for a hot, scratchy throat.`,buy:`Dried honeysuckle flowers that are still pale yellow and lightly scented. Brown dust has usually lost the point.`,how:`Steep 6–20 g in hot water, or add it near the end of a simmer. Tea bags are the form most people buy.`,range:`Textbook amount: 6–20 g of dried flowers.`,look:`Dried honeysuckle flowers, pale yellow.`},"shi-gao":{short:`A mineral for high-fever pictures. Not a home fever remedy.`,what:`Gypsum is a mineral, not a plant. Textbooks use large amounts for a pattern of high fever, big thirst, and blazing heat. It is cooked first and for a long time. It is not something to take because you feel a bit warm.`,feel:`Very cold and heavy. It clears intense heat. There is no aroma and no plant taste, just a mineral.`,who:`Students learning why some ingredients are simmered before the leaves and flowers. Not for self-treating a fever.`,buy:`Food- or medicine-grade gypsum chunks from an herb supplier, labeled shi gao. Never use wall plaster, drywall, or industrial gypsum.`,how:`Break 15–60 g into small pieces and simmer them first for 20–30 minutes before other herbs go in. This is a practitioner formula ingredient.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 15–60 g, cooked first. The wide range is a classroom note, not a slider you set at home.`,look:`Gypsum chunks, the white mineral used in decoctions.`,alert:`Do not self-treat a fever with gypsum. High fever needs medical care. Never substitute building plaster.`},"zhi-mu":{short:`A root for heat that has also made the body dry.`,what:`Used when heat and dryness show up together: thirst, a dry mouth, restlessness, or a hot dry cough in textbook language. It is often paired with gypsum because this root also moistens.`,feel:`Cold, bitter, and a little sweet. It clears heat without only drying you out.`,who:`Students of heat-plus-dryness, and people comparing the yellow heat-clearing herbs.`,buy:`Dried anemarrhena slices, flat and yellowish. An old quality note says good slices feel slightly sticky when chewed.`,how:`Simmer 6–12 g for about 20 minutes. Clinic formulas often use granules.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–12 g a day.`,look:`Dried anemarrhena slices, pale yellow.`},"fu-ling":{short:`A mild fungus for a heavy, waterlogged feeling.`,what:`Poria is a fungus that grows around pine roots. It is the ordinary herb for a heavy, puffy, or foggy feeling that traditional texts call damp. It also shows up in mild sleep formulas because it is gentle.`,feel:`Neutral, bland, and slightly sweet. It drains damp without being harsh. Easy to combine.`,who:`People who feel heavy, bloated, or waterlogged and want the mildest herb in that group.`,buy:`White poria slices or cubes. They should look pale and dry, not moldy. Red poria is a related cut with a different emphasis.`,how:`Simmer 9–15 g for about 20 minutes, or cook the cubes in soup. The taste is mild. Granules are common.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 9–15 g a day.`,look:`White poria slices, the dried fungus.`},"ze-xie":{short:`A draining tuber for puffiness and cloudy water.`,what:`Alisma is used when traditional texts describe too much water in the system: puffiness, or urine that is scanty or cloudy. It drains more actively than poria. It does not treat kidney disease.`,feel:`Cold, sweet, and bland. It drains downward.`,who:`Students of damp-draining formulas, usually next to poria.`,buy:`Dried alisma slices, off-white and starchy, from an herb shop.`,how:`Simmer about 6–12 g for 20 minutes. It is usually inside a formula, not a flavored tea.`,range:`Textbook decoction: about 6–12 g a day.`,look:`Dried alisma tuber slices.`},"yi-yi-ren":{short:`Job’s tears, a grain-like seed for dampness.`,what:`Coix seed is both a food and an herb for dampness: heavy limbs, loose stools, or a lingering damp ache in the joints in textbook notes. People cook it the way they cook a grain.`,feel:`Cool, sweet, and bland. Gentle. It leaches damp rather than forcing it.`,who:`People who want a kitchen form of a damp-draining herb.`,buy:`Dried coix seeds, sometimes called Job’s tears. They look a bit like pearl barley, but they are not barley. Skip sweetened canned drinks if you are trying to study the herb.`,how:`Cook 9–30 g as porridge, or simmer 30–40 minutes in a decoction. The higher end is the food-like use. It takes longer to soften than a leaf.`,range:`Textbook amount: 9–30 g a day. Food-style cooking sits toward the top of that range.`,look:`Dried coix seeds, also called Job’s tears.`},"ban-xia":{short:`Processed pinellia for nausea. Raw pinellia is toxic.`,what:`Pinellia is used for nausea and for thick phlegm in the stomach or chest. Only the processed form is ever used in medicine. Raw pinellia irritates the mouth and throat and is toxic. Do not prepare it from a plant you picked.`,feel:`After proper processing it is warm, spicy, and drying. It is said to transform phlegm. Unprocessed, it is caustic, not a spice.`,who:`Students of nausea formulas. Not a do-it-yourself herb.`,buy:`Only processed pinellia, labeled fa ban xia, jiang ban xia, or a similar prepared name, from a reputable supplier. If the label does not say processed, do not buy it.`,how:`Textbooks simmer the processed slices, often with fresh ginger, which is also the traditional way to reduce harshness. Granules from a clinic are the safer way to study it. Never use the raw tuber.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g of processed pinellia a day. Raw pinellia has no home dose.`,look:`Processed pinellia slices, the only form used in medicine.`,alert:`Raw pinellia is toxic. Only processed ban xia belongs in a formula, and only with a qualified practitioner. Do not cook the fresh plant.`},"chen-pi":{short:`Aged tangerine peel for a full, stuck stomach.`,what:`Aged tangerine peel is the kitchen herb for a stomach that feels full and stuck: bloating, a poor appetite, or food that just sits. It also helps thin mucus that comes with a weak digestion.`,feel:`Warm, aromatic, and slightly bitter. It wakes up the middle of the body.`,who:`People with ordinary fullness after eating who want a traditional peel tea.`,buy:`Dried aged citrus peel, orange-brown and fragrant. Chen pi means it has been aged. A waxed fresh orange peel from the grocery is not the same thing.`,how:`Steep or simmer 3–9 g for about 10 minutes, often after meals. Tea and granules are both common. The smell should be clearly citrus.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Aged tangerine peel, curled and orange-brown.`},"hou-po":{short:`Magnolia bark for a chest or belly that feels blocked.`,what:`Magnolia bark is stronger than tangerine peel for a chest or belly that feels blocked, full, and heavy. Textbook notes also mention it when dampness sits with a cough. It moves, it does not sedate.`,feel:`Warm, bitter, and aromatic. It is for a stuck, full feeling.`,who:`Students of fullness formulas, often beside citrus peel.`,buy:`Thick dried magnolia bark, sometimes rolled, with a spicy smell. Magnolia flower tea is a different product.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for 15–20 minutes, usually with other herbs rather than as a solo drink.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried magnolia bark, thick and aromatic.`},"xiang-fu":{short:`An aromatic rhizome for a stuck chest, ribs, or cycle.`,what:`Cyperus is used when stress shows up as a stuck chest, tightness along the ribs, or crampy cycle discomfort in traditional language. It moves qi. It does not treat depression or replace menstrual care.`,feel:`Neutral to slightly warm, spicy, and a little sweet and bitter. Students learn it as a qi mover for the sides of the body.`,who:`People looking up stress-related fullness or cycle cramps in traditional notes.`,buy:`Dried cyperus rhizome, small brown pieces that still smell fragrant. Vinegar-fried slices are a common prepared form in shops.`,how:`Simmer 6–12 g for about 15 minutes. Granules are widely used in clinic formulas.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–12 g a day.`,look:`Dried cyperus rhizome, the aromatic nutgrass pieces.`},"chai-hu":{short:`Bupleurum for a pattern stuck between a chill and a fever.`,what:`Bupleurum is the herb for a pattern that is neither fully on the surface nor fully inside: alternating chills and warmth, a bitter taste, rib-side tightness, and a mood that feels snagged. Small amounts are also used to gently lift.`,feel:`Cool, bitter, and slightly spicy. It is aimed at the sides of the rib cage.`,who:`Anyone who has heard of Xiao Chai Hu Tang, or who feels stress as tightness under the ribs.`,buy:`Dried bupleurum root, thin sticks or slices, from an herb supplier. A “liver detox” blend is not this herb.`,how:`Simmer 3–12 g. Textbooks use the lower end, around 3–6 g, when the goal is lifting, and more when the goal is harmonizing a half-outside pattern. It is usually combined.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–12 g a day. The job of the formula decides where you sit in that range.`,look:`Dried bupleurum root, thin brown sticks.`},"mu-xiang":{short:`A strong aromatic root for cramping fullness in the belly.`,what:`Used for a belly that feels stuck and uncomfortable in a moving way: gas, crampy fullness, and a poor appetite. It moves digestion. It does not numb severe pain, and severe pain needs a clinician.`,feel:`Warm, spicy, bitter, and very aromatic. The smell is the point, so it is not boiled for a long time.`,who:`Students of digestive qi movers, often next to citrus peel or cyperus.`,buy:`Dried aucklandia or a legal cultivated mu xiang. Some costus species are endangered. Buy from a supplier that sells a lawful cultivated root, not wild illegal stock.`,how:`Add 3–9 g near the end and simmer only 5–10 minutes. Granules are the other usual form.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g, added late.`,look:`Dried mu xiang root slices, dark and aromatic.`},"chuan-xiong":{short:`A moving root for stuck headaches and cycle pain.`,what:`A warm, moving root for headaches and body aches that feel stuck, and for cycle pain in the traditional language of moving blood. It is one of the first herbs students learn for the head. It is not a painkiller you can stack freely.`,feel:`Warm, spicy, and strongly moving. Texts say it moves both qi and blood.`,who:`People looking up headaches or period cramps in traditional formulas.`,buy:`Dried ligusticum slices, brown and aromatic, labeled chuan xiong. It is not the same plant as dang gui.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for about 15 minutes, usually in a formula or as granules. Moving herbs are traditionally avoided in pregnancy unless a prescriber chooses them.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried chuan xiong slices, irregular and aromatic.`,alert:`Traditionally avoided in pregnancy unless a licensed practitioner includes it. It can be too moving for heavy bleeding.`},"dan-shen":{short:`Red sage root for stuck circulation language in the chest.`,what:`Red sage root is used when traditional texts describe stuck circulation in the chest, restlessness, or menstrual discomfort linked to blood stasis. It is not a substitute for heart medicine, and it can interact with blood thinners.`,feel:`Cool and bitter. It moves blood and is said to cool a restless feeling.`,who:`Students of chest and blood-moving formulas. Anyone on blood pressure or blood-thinning drugs should ask before adding it.`,buy:`Dark red salvia root slices. The cut face looks reddish. A standardized extract capsule is a modern product, stronger and less predictable than a traditional slice.`,how:`Simmer 6–15 g for about 20 minutes, or use practitioner granules. Do not add it on top of heart or anticoagulant medicine without advice.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried red salvia root, the reddish slices called dan shen.`},"hong-hua":{short:`Safflower, a red blossom that moves blood. Not saffron.`,what:`Safflower is used in small amounts to move blood: fixed bruises and stuck cycle pain in textbook language. It is not cooking saffron, and it is not for pregnancy.`,feel:`Warm and spicy. A blood mover. The color of a decoction often turns orange-red.`,who:`Students of blood-moving formulas. Not a self-chosen herb in pregnancy.`,buy:`Dried safflower florets, bright red-orange. Saffron threads are a different plant and a kitchen spice, not a substitute.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for about 10 minutes so the color is not destroyed. It is usually a smaller part of a formula.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried safflower florets. These are not saffron.`,alert:`Not for self-use in pregnancy. It is a moving herb, not a culinary garnish.`},"tao-ren":{short:`Peach kernels that move blood. Not a snack.`,what:`Peach kernel is used to move blood and also to moisten the intestines, so texts mention it for stuck pain together with dry constipation. The kernel is not a snack. Eating a pile of raw stone-fruit pits is unsafe.`,feel:`Neutral, bitter, and sweet. Moving, and slightly moistening for dry stools.`,who:`Students. Not someone looking for a trail-mix ingredient.`,buy:`Peach kernels from an herb shop, often already prepared for decoction. Do not use a bag of raw peach pits or bitter almonds.`,how:`Crush 4.5–9 g and simmer about 15 minutes, inside a formula. Do not eat them by the handful.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 4.5–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried peach kernels, the medicinal seeds, not fresh pits to eat.`,alert:`Do not eat raw peach kernels as food. Medicinal use stays inside a practitioner formula.`},"yan-hu-suo":{short:`Corydalis, the tuber texts use for stuck pain.`,what:`Corydalis is the pain herb in this set. Texts use it for pain from stuck qi and blood: stomachache, cycle pain, and fixed aches. It is not an opioid, and severe or new pain needs medical care.`,feel:`Warm, spicy, and bitter. In traditional language it both moves and eases pain.`,who:`People studying traditional pain formulas, especially vinegar-fried slices.`,buy:`Dried corydalis slices. Vinegar-fried slices are the common shop form, and textbooks say the vinegar preparation fits the pain use.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for 15–20 minutes, or use prepared granules. It is usually combined with other movers such as chuan xiong.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried corydalis slices, often the vinegar-prepared form.`},"ren-shen":{short:`Ginseng for deep tiredness. Not an energy shot.`,what:`Ginseng is the famous root for deep tiredness, a weak appetite, and a feeling of being emptied out. It is strong. Old books mention very large amounts for collapse. Those are emergency notes, not a home plan.`,feel:`Warm, sweet, and slightly bitter. It strongly builds qi. Too much can feel agitating, hot, or tight in the chest.`,who:`People comparing true ginseng with astragalus, which is milder. Anyone on several medicines should ask first.`,buy:`Dried Panax ginseng root or slices. Red ginseng has been steamed and is warmer. American ginseng is a cooler cousin and a different herb. Skip unnamed energy blends.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g of slices slowly for 30–40 minutes. A few slices as a mild tea is the ordinary study amount. Capsule extracts vary wildly in strength, so the slice range is the one to learn.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day. Do not copy collapse-level megadoses for home use.`,look:`Dried ginseng root, the form sold as ren shen.`},"huang-qi":{short:`Astragalus, the everyday root for being run down.`,what:`Astragalus is the milder tonic for tiredness, catching every mild cold, and a poor appetite. It is one of the roots people actually cook in soup. It is not a cure for immune disease.`,feel:`Slightly warm and sweet. In traditional language it lifts and firms qi.`,who:`People who feel run down and want to understand the long pale slices in herb shops.`,buy:`Dried astragalus root slices, long, pale yellow, and sweet-smelling. Look for the root, not a sweetened bottled drink, if you are studying the herb.`,how:`Simmer 9–15 g, or up to 30 g in some textbook tonic uses, for about 30 minutes in water or soup. Tea bags and granules are common. Traditional texts say to pause it during an acute fever.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 9–30 g a day. Everyday tonic use is often 9–15 g.`,look:`Dried astragalus root slices, long and pale yellow.`},"bai-zhu":{short:`A root that supports a weak, damp digestion.`,what:`White atractylodes is used when the appetite is low, stools are loose, and the body feels heavy and tired. Traditional language calls it a spleen tonic. It is often cooked with poria.`,feel:`Warm, bitter, and sweet. It dries damp and supports digestion.`,who:`People with a weak, bloated digestion who are reading classic tonic formulas.`,buy:`Dried Atractylodes macrocephala slices, irregular and yellowish. The darker herb cang zhu is a different, more drying species.`,how:`Simmer 6–15 g for 20–30 minutes. Soup and granules are both common.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried white atractylodes slices.`},"gan-cao":{short:`Licorice root, the sweet herb that softens a formula.`,what:`Licorice is the harmonizer. A little is added to countless formulas to soften harsh herbs and to ease a dry throat or a crampy abdomen. It is not candy. Large amounts for a long time can raise blood pressure and disturb potassium.`,feel:`Neutral and very sweet. In small amounts it moderates the other herbs. Honey-fried licorice is the softer, more tonic prepared form.`,who:`Anyone who sees it in almost every classical formula and wants to know why it is there.`,buy:`Dried licorice sticks or slices. Zhi gan cao means honey-fried. DGL tablets are a modern product made to remove the constituent that affects blood pressure. They are not the same as the traditional root.`,how:`1.5–9 g in a decoction. When it is only there to harmonize, formulas stay near the low end. Do not drink strong licorice tea every day on your own.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 1.5–9 g a day. Long, high use is the safety problem.`,look:`Dried licorice root sticks.`,alert:`Long or high use can raise blood pressure and lower potassium. Skip it if you have high blood pressure unless a clinician says otherwise.`},"dang-gui":{short:`The aromatic root people look up for cycle depletion.`,what:`Chinese angelica is the blood tonic associated with cycle irregularity, a pale dry pattern, and aches that traditional texts treat by nourishing and gently moving blood. It is not hormone replacement.`,feel:`Warm, sweet, and spicy. It both nourishes and gently moves. The smell is strong, sweet, and celery-like.`,who:`People looking up traditional support around the menstrual cycle. Not for self-use in pregnancy.`,buy:`Aromatic brown dang gui slices. Shops may sell the body or the tail. Bai zhi is a different angelica used for the face, not this one.`,how:`Simmer 4.5–15 g for about 20 minutes, or use granules. It is often combined with white peony and prepared rehmannia.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 4.5–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried dang gui slices, brown and strongly aromatic.`,alert:`Not a self-chosen herb in pregnancy. Moving blood tonics belong with a qualified practitioner.`},"shu-di":{short:`Dark, rich rehmannia for deep dryness. Heavy on the stomach.`,what:`Prepared rehmannia is a dark, soft, very rich root for deep dryness and blood-depletion pictures: pale, dry, light-headed, or cycle depletion in old language. It is heavy. A weak, loose digestion often disagrees with it.`,feel:`Slightly warm and sweet. Thick and cloying. It nourishes yin and blood rather than moving them.`,who:`Students of blood and yin tonics. People who already have loose stools should not pick it as a casual tea.`,buy:`Soft, black, shiny slices of prepared rehmannia. Raw rehmannia is colder, lighter in color, and is a different herb.`,how:`Simmer 9–30 g for about 30 minutes. Texts often cook it with ginger or tangerine peel so it is less heavy on the stomach. Granules are easier to take.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 9–30 g a day.`,look:`Prepared rehmannia, the black shiny slices called shu di.`},"bai-shao":{short:`White peony for cramps and tightness.`,what:`White peony softens tightness. Texts use it for crampy pain, rib-side tension, and for protecting the nourishing side of the body when cinnamon twig is warming the outside. It is the sour partner in many women’s formulas.`,feel:`Cool, sour, and a little bitter. It softens spasms in traditional language and is gentler than the moving roots.`,who:`People reading about crampy cycles or tight muscles, and students of the cinnamon-and-peony pair.`,buy:`Pale white peony root slices. Red peony is a different herb that moves blood more and nourishes less.`,how:`Simmer 6–15 g for about 20 minutes. It is very common in granules and in prepared women’s formulas.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried white peony root slices.`},"gou-qi-zi":{short:`Goji berries, a food-herb for tired eyes and dryness.`,what:`Goji berries are a food-herb for tired eyes, dryness, and a mildly depleted feeling. They do not repair vision or treat eye disease. People eat them in soup, oatmeal, and tea.`,feel:`Neutral and sweet. Traditional texts say they gently support the liver and kidney, which is why they are paired with chrysanthemum for the eyes.`,who:`Anyone who already snacks on goji and wants the traditional note, not a supplement claim.`,buy:`Whole dried red berries that are not coated in sugar. A kitchen bag is the right form for food use.`,how:`6–12 g a day in tea, porridge, or soup. Steeping is enough. You do not need a long medicinal boil.`,range:`Textbook amount: 6–12 g a day, which is also an ordinary food portion.`,look:`Dried goji berries, the red fruit people eat and steep.`},"mai-men-dong":{short:`A small tuber for a dry throat, lungs, or stomach.`,what:`Ophiopogon is used when the throat, lungs, or stomach feel dry: a dry cough, thirst, or restlessness from dryness in textbook language. It moistens. It is not a cough suppressant.`,feel:`Cool, sweet, and slightly bitter. It nourishes fluids.`,who:`People with a dry-throat pattern looking up a moistening herb.`,buy:`Small spindle-shaped tubers, pale yellow and a little chewy, often labeled mai dong.`,how:`Simmer 6–15 g for about 20 minutes, or steep them as a mild tea. Often paired with other moistening herbs rather than used as a strong solo dose.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried ophiopogon tubers, small and spindle-shaped.`},"du-zhong":{short:`Eucommia bark for a weak, achy low back.`,what:`Eucommia bark is the low-back herb in this set. Texts use it when the waist and knees feel weak and cold. Some traditional pregnancy formulas include it, but only under a prescriber. It is not a do-it-yourself pregnancy herb.`,feel:`Warm, sweet, and slightly spicy. Traditional language says it supports the lower back and sinews.`,who:`People looking up a tired, achy low back. Pregnancy questions go to a licensed herbalist.`,buy:`Dried eucommia bark. A shop-floor quality check is the fine white threads that appear when a piece is snapped. Those threads are the signature of the bark.`,how:`Simmer 9–15 g for 20–30 minutes. Pills and granules aimed at the low back are the other form people buy.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 9–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried eucommia bark. Snapped pieces show fine white threads.`},"rou-gui":{short:`Cinnamon bark for deep cold. Hotter than the twig.`,what:`This is the thick cinnamon bark, not the twig. It is hotter. Texts use a little to warm deep cold: cold limbs, a cold lower abdomen, and low-back weakness in yang-deficiency language. A small amount is enough.`,feel:`Hot, sweet, and spicy. It warms the interior. Wrong choice if you already feel hot, flushed, or dried out.`,who:`People who run cold and are comparing bark (rou gui) with twig (gui zhi).`,buy:`Thick rolled cassia bark quills, oily and sweet-smelling. Kitchen cinnamon is related, but it is not a measured stand-in for the medicinal bark.`,how:`Add 1.5–4.5 g near the end of a simmer, or steep it briefly. A long boil drives off the aroma and is not how texts use it.`,range:`Textbook amount: 1.5–4.5 g, added near the end.`,look:`Cinnamon bark quills, thicker and hotter than cinnamon twigs.`},"suan-zao-ren":{short:`Sour jujube seeds, the sleep herb in this set.`,what:`Sour jujube seed is the sleep herb students learn first. It is used when someone cannot settle at night, especially with dryness or depletion rather than only a racing schedule. It is a seed, often crushed. It is not a sleeping pill.`,feel:`Neutral, sweet, and sour. It nourishes and calms in traditional language.`,who:`People looking up traditional sleep support. Ongoing insomnia still deserves a real clinical conversation.`,buy:`Small reddish-brown seeds labeled suan zao ren. Fried seeds are a common prepared form. The jujube fruit, da zao, is a different herb.`,how:`Crush 9–18 g and simmer about 20 minutes. Traditional schedules often take it in the evening. Powders and pills are widely sold. Ask before combining it with sleep or mood medicines.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 9–18 g a day.`,look:`Dried sour jujube seeds, not the red fruit.`},"yuan-zhi":{short:`A small root for worry with poor sleep. Use the processed form.`,what:`Polygala is used when worry and poor sleep go together, and when old texts describe phlegm clouding the mind. It is often paired with sour jujube seed. The raw root irritates the throat, so shops sell it processed.`,feel:`Slightly warm, bitter, and spicy. It calms and also transforms phlegm in textbook language.`,who:`Students of spirit-calming formulas. People with a sore throat should avoid the unprocessed root.`,buy:`Processed polygala, often honey-fried or prepared with licorice, labeled yuan zhi. The woody core is sometimes removed because it is more irritating.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for about 15 minutes. It is usually combined, not taken as a strong tea by itself.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day of the processed root.`,look:`Processed polygala root, often with the core removed.`},"long-gu":{short:`Fossil bone, a heavy mineral used to settle restlessness.`,what:`Dragon bone is fossilized bone used to settle a restless, unanchored feeling: palpitations, light sleep, or sweat that will not stay in, in textbook language. It is cooked first. It is not a calcium supplement plan, and it is not heart treatment.`,feel:`Neutral, sweet, and astringent. Heavy and settling. No plant aroma.`,who:`Students of anchoring herbs. A racing heart needs medical care, not a mineral tea.`,buy:`Pale fossil-bone chunks from an herb supplier, labeled long gu. Not a curiosity-shop fossil, and not livestock bone meal.`,how:`Simmer 15–30 g first for 20–30 minutes, then add lighter herbs. Clinic granules exist. This is a formula ingredient more than a drink.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 15–30 g, cooked first.`,look:`Pale fossil-bone chunks, the mineral called long gu.`},"tian-ma":{short:`Gastrodia for dizziness in textbook language. Not stroke care.`,what:`Gastrodia is used for dizziness, a heavy or windy head, and headache pictures that texts call internal wind. It does not diagnose or treat stroke, inner-ear disease, or seizures. New dizziness is a reason to see a clinician.`,feel:`Neutral and sweet. Traditional language says it calms wind.`,who:`Students of dizziness formulas, often beside uncaria hooks.`,buy:`Dried gastrodia slices, translucent and horn-like. Wild plants are protected. Buy cultivated material from a proper supplier.`,how:`Simmer 3–9 g for about 20 minutes. Prepared pills are the other form people meet. Do not delay care for real vertigo or a sudden headache.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 3–9 g a day.`,look:`Dried gastrodia slices, translucent and horn-like.`,alert:`Sudden dizziness, weakness, or a severe headache is an emergency, not an herb problem.`},"gou-teng":{short:`Hooks of a vine for head tension. Add them at the end.`,what:`Uncaria hooks are a lighter herb for headache, a tight jaw, or irritability in a wind picture. The curved hooks are the part that is used. They go in at the end of cooking. This is not a blood-pressure treatment to run on your own.`,feel:`Cool and sweet. Texts say it extinguishes wind and clears a little heat.`,who:`Students pairing it with gastrodia for head tension and dizziness notes.`,buy:`Dried stem pieces that still have curved hooks. The hooks matter more than a bag of plain stems.`,how:`Add 6–15 g in the last 10 minutes. Do not boil the hooks for half an hour. That is the practical detail people miss.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–15 g, added at the end.`,look:`Dried uncaria stems with the curved hooks still attached.`},"wu-wei-zi":{short:`Schisandra berries, sour herbs that hold a leaky pattern.`,what:`Schisandra is used when traditional texts want to hold something that is leaking: sweat, a weak cough, or a scattered, tired feeling. The berry tastes sour. It is not an energy drink.`,feel:`Warm, and famous for containing all five flavors, though sour is what you notice. It stabilizes.`,who:`People who see the dark berries in teas and want the traditional holding use, not a marketing claim.`,buy:`Whole dried schisandra berries, dark red, wrinkled, and very sour.`,how:`Crush 3–6 g and simmer about 15 minutes, or steep a few berries. The range stays small because the berry is strong. Capsule extracts vary and are a different product.`,range:`Textbook decoction: about 3–6 g a day.`,look:`Dried schisandra berries, dark red and wrinkled.`},"shan-zhu-yu":{short:`A sour fruit for a weak lower back in holding formulas.`,what:`Cornus fruit is sour and is used to hold the lower body in textbook language: a weak low back, or urinary frequency in a depleted pattern. It does not treat diabetes or incontinence.`,feel:`Slightly warm and sour. Astringent, and also somewhat nourishing.`,who:`Students of kidney-holding formulas, often beside prepared rehmannia.`,buy:`Dried cornus fruit flesh, dark red and wrinkled, usually with the seed removed.`,how:`Simmer 6–12 g for about 20 minutes. People meet it more often in prepared pills than as a tea on its own.`,range:`Textbook decoction: 6–12 g a day.`,look:`Dried cornus fruit, dark and wrinkled.`},"da-zao":{short:`Chinese dates. A food that softens a formula.`,what:`The Chinese date, or jujube fruit, is a food that harmonizes formulas, supports a weak stomach, and makes a harsh decoction easier to drink. This is the fruit. The sleep herb is the seed, suan zao ren, not this.`,feel:`Warm and sweet. Like a food: it mildly supports qi and blood in traditional language.`,who:`Anyone making a traditional tea who wants the fruit that shows up beside ginger.`,buy:`Dried red jujubes, whole and wrinkled. Grocery dried jujubes are the right food form.`,how:`Split 3 to 10 fruits, about 6–15 g, and simmer 15–20 minutes with other herbs or as a mild tea. This is the most kitchen-like herb in the set.`,range:`Textbook amount: a few dates, or about 6–15 g.`,look:`Dried Chinese dates, the red jujube fruit.`},"yin-yang-huo":{short:`Epimedium leaf for a cold, depleted pattern. Not a performance pill.`,what:`Epimedium leaf is a yang tonic in textbooks: a cold low back, low vitality, and sexual tiredness in traditional language. Marketing sometimes turns it into a performance pill. That is the wrong use, and it is the wrong herb if you already feel hot and dry.`,feel:`Warm, spicy, and sweet. It is said to support yang and ease cold-type joint pain.`,who:`Students of yang tonics who want the leaf, not the headline on a bottle.`,buy:`Dried epimedium leaves that still look like leaves, olive to brown. Be wary of unnamed high-dose extracts sold for sex.`,how:`Simmer about 6–15 g for 15–20 minutes, usually in a formula. Do not stack it with sexual-enhancement products or blood-pressure drugs on your own.`,range:`Textbook decoction: about 6–15 g a day.`,look:`Dried epimedium leaves, the herb itself rather than a capsule.`,alert:`Not a sexual-performance product. Skip it with heart disease, hormone-sensitive conditions, or other performance pills unless a clinician is directing care.`}};for(let e of T)if(!D[e.id])throw Error(`Missing plain-language note for ${e.id}`);function O(e){return D[e]??null}export{d as C,u as D,m as E,p as S,l as T,e as _,C as a,r as b,y as c,v as d,g as f,n as g,i as h,S as i,o as l,b as m,O as n,w as o,c as p,T as r,x as s,E as t,_ as u,a as v,h as w,f as x,t as y};